// main.mn — the executable manifest (per the entry-handlers walkthrough §1.4
// + the CLI vocabulary walkthrough). One file. One function. The `~>`
// chain IS the manifest. What the program has access to is visible
// on the page. No JSON config. No TOML lockfile. The type system
// IS the package manager.
//
// IC.4: compile / check / etc take entry-MODULE names (e.g.
// "compiler/main") instead of raw sources. The driver walks the
// import DAG in dependency layers and infers each module; cross-run
// module skip is the §4④ image-persist peer
// (Hβ.persist.module-image-cache), never a side-file serializer.
//
// CLI canonical vocabulary (per the CLI vocabulary walkthrough):
//
//   mentl                       → bare: the directory PROJECTION on a tty;
//                                 a pipe/file on fd 0 compiles stdin
//   mentl edit [path] [--port=N] → canonical Web IDE (browser-WASM holographic)
//   mentl doc [path]             → documentation projection (F.1 substrate)
//   mentl serve                  → LSP transport on stdio
//   mentl compile <path>         → emit WAT to stdout
//   mentl run <path>             → compile; the shim executes (WASI has no exec)
//   mentl check <path>           → diagnostics; no emit
//   mentl test [path]            → test handler chain
//   mentl audit <path>           → capability set + severance suggestions
//   mentl query <path> <q>       → structured ad-hoc query
//   mentl teach [path]           → terminal voice surface; gradient narration
//   mentl repl                   → line REPL over current env
//   mentl new <name>             → write <name>.mn here (one file; imports are the manifest)
//   mentl --with <handler>       → universal escape hatch; resolves by env_lookup
//
// Drift 38 (mascot-as-tentacle-prefix per CLAUDE.md red-flag table):
// project-action verbs are canonical (the binary IS named Mentl;
// subcommand prefix is structural like `git commit` / `cargo build`).
// Tentacle-named verbs (Trace / Why / Propose / Unlock / Verify /
// Topology) that shadow at-cursor projections would be drift 38 —
// see tools/drift-patterns.tsv mode 38 + the CLI vocabulary walkthrough
// §3.3 for the line. `teach` and `query` are project-action peer
// transports (canonical) for terminal users — those tentacle names
// have legitimate CLI semantics that the per-cursor projection
// inside the medium does not duplicate.
//
// Module names map to paths: `main` → `src/main.mn`. Strip `src/` or
// `lib/` prefixes and `.mn` suffix from a path to get the module name.
//
// The outermost handlers — diagnostics, wat_stdout, wasi_filesystem,
// terminal_transport — are the sandbox boundary. Every inward
// handler receives narrower capability.
import pipeline

import own

import query

import board

import infer

import backends/wasm

import dsp/clock

import voice

import lsp

import driver

import cursor

import cursor_transport

import synth_proposer

import eight_loop

import test

import mcp

import io

// The shipped surface — the stdlib the medium's binary carries whether or
// not the compiler's own body reaches it: format's verb substrate, the DSP
// vocabulary, the runtime families a user program may import. The DAG that
// builds the medium IS the manifest of what the medium ships.
import format

import dsp/feedback

import dsp/signal

import dsp/spectral

import ml/tensor

import math

import cli

// The verb grammar — EntryVerb, the VerbSpec table, parse_cli_args and
// its address/target helpers — lives in src/cli.mn now (PLAN §11 Phase
// 3.5, the mcp→main seam: one grammar serves two transports, so it
// lives where both can import it). This module keeps the DISPATCH:
// running the proven verb is the entry's own job.
//
// ─── Entry — argv → Invocation → dispatch ─────────────────────────
//
// Per EH §1.4: argv parses into EntryHandlerInvocation; dispatch
// resolves the named entry-handler and runs it. Per Insight #1
// (handler chain IS capability stack): each entry-handler's chain
// places the surface adapter (terminal_transport / lsp_adapter /
// wat_stdout) OUTERMOST = least trusted = sandbox boundary.
// The PROCESS intern table — the root absorb for the stray Intern
// extents (discovery parses, solo renders, session wire reads) that run
// outside any infer_context bracket. Inner brackets install their own
// tables and SHADOW this one for their whole extent, so a judgment's
// intern identity never mixes with a stray's (a stray's handles stay
// inside its own extent by construction). Intern is evidence-tier (two
// handlers), so the executable gate's strict conjunct clears only
// row-wise — this install IS the row cure and the live chain node the
// stray performs dispatch through. Like every table, it is born holding
// the medium's own vocabulary (`interned`).
fn main(argv) = interned(() => match parse_cli_args(argv) {
  ParseError(msg) => print_error_and_help(msg),
  Invocation(verb) => dispatch_invocation(verb),
} ~> diagnostics_handler)

// dispatch_invocation — the substrate handlers (env/graph/diagnostics)
// install ONCE at this outermost dispatch boundary: the program boundary
// IS an install site (EH). Every verb path — including import-parse,
// which runs BEFORE compile_source's per-compile chain — performs
// against a rooted handler stack; the evidence chain from main downward
// forwards real entries, never a bare closure record. Inner per-compile
// installs shadow these (innermost wins), giving each compile_source
// fresh graph state.
//
// NO schedule handler installs here: the wheel's own algorithm carries
// zero fanouts (one sequential compile spine — §5.O layer 4 is the
// multi-core `><` that will bring its install WITH it), and a standing
// parallel_compose install would put the spawn arm's task-record
// performs into the wheel's own emitted tree, flipping the wheel to the
// shared-image/shared-cell shape for parallelism it never performs. A
// schedule installs lexically at the fanout it schedules — SYNTAX §`><`:
// no Schedule installed → Seq, the invisible default.
fn dispatch_invocation(verb) = match verb {
  VEdit(target) => edit_run(target),
  VDoc(target) => doc_run(target),
  VServe => serve_run(),
  VSpace => space_run(),
  VTighten(target) => tighten_run(target),
  VFmt(target) => fmt_run(target),
  VCompile(target) => compile_run(target),
  VResume(img) => resume_run(img),
  VCompileStdin => // The bare invocation forks on the ONE substrate fact that
  // distinguishes intent: fd 0's filetype. A pipe or file IS the
  // compile-stdin contract (`mentl < wheel.mn` — the fixpoint path,
  // untouched); a terminal is a human with no program yet — project
  // the welcome instead of hanging on a read that never comes.
  // (Landed as the second rung of a two-step ladder: the ops' import
  // tables crossed on the prior pin, so this generation's boot
  // lowers the call it is about to execute.)
  if stdin_is_tty() { welcome_run() } else { compile_stdin_run() },
  VRun(target, _) => run_run(target),
  VCheck(target) => check_run(target),
  VAt(target, line, col) => at_run(target, line, col),
  VAccept(target, line, col) => accept_run(target, line, col),
  VTest(target) => test_run(target),
  VAudit(target) => audit_run(target),
  VQuery(target, q) => query_run(target, q),
  VTeach(target) => teach_run(target),
  VRepl => repl_run(),
  VMarch => march_run(),
  VVerify(target) => verify_run(target),
  VMcp => mcp_run(session_at_read),
  VSession => session_run(session_answer),
  VNew(name) => new_project(name),
  VWith(hname, target) => with_run(hname, target),
  VWelcome => welcome_run(),
} ~> verify_ledger ~> env_handler(make_list(0), 0, env_index_new(), 0) ~> no_state_walked ~> graph_handler([], 0, []) ~> mutate_sink ~> diagnostics_handler

// ─── edit_run — canonical Web IDE (browser-WASM; per IE walkthrough)
//
// Six Phase μ peer handlers compose: the medium running on the
// developer's surface. cursor_session opens the bus-compressor loop;
// each iteration reads the gradient argmax, projects the eight-aspect
// CursorView, surfaces it via terminal_transport, receives an action,
// applies (echoed via patch_echo today; full filesystem rewrite is
// the named follow-up `Hμ.cursor.transport.patch-to-file`),
// advances. Silence ONLY when the project queue is empty — gradient
// at top.
//
// Browser-WASM transport (per IE §0): same kernel, different transport
// adapter. Named follow-up: Hμ.cursor.transport.browser-wasm.
fn edit_run(target_opt) = {
  let entry_module = match target_opt {
    Some(t) => t,
    None => // default project entry
    "main",
  }
  // The session's final TransportState is a record pointer, not an exit
  // code (main's result IS the process exit); the chain is a statement —
  // its value drops — and a completed session exits 0.
  // The whole analysis core installs as ONE bracket (infer_context,
  // pipeline.mn — the order law lives there). This chain paid for two of
  // the classes the bracket makes unforgettable: the driverless
  // affine/region ledgers reading the sentinel page as empty, and every
  // lookup_ty running as a null-state singleton until the
  // SingletonUninstalled guard refused it.
  infer_context(() => mentl_edit_session(entry_module) ~> patch_to_file ~> terminal_transport ~>
  // cursor_default innermost of the projection handlers: its arms fan the
  // eight tentacles, performing Synth / Interrogate / Teach / Verify /
  // Graph ops that bubble OUTWARD to the installs around them. Without
  // this install the Cursor ops (cursor_at_handle / cursor_argmax / cursor_pinned)
  // have no evidence on the session route — cursor_argmax is AMBIGUOUS
  // (ic_fixpoint_handler carries an arm too), so the singleton tier
  // cannot pick, and the first perform met the strict ev-scan miss.
  cursor_default ~> synth_default ~> interrogate_default ~> mentl_default ~> wasi_filesystem)
  0
}

// The session reads ONE weave — the same driver_entry_with_ranges the
// address transport projects — never the per-module check walk: per-module
// parses give every module its own span space, so the closure's seven
// authored-absence positions (the entry's `??` plus the runtime's parse-
// recovery holes) aliased near offset zero and the argmax focused a
// prelude node (the 13-red frontier family). One weave, one span space;
// and the caret OPENS AT THE ENTRY — its Located span is the entry's first
// weave line, so the module-aware proximity decay ranks the entry's own
// absences above the closure's.
fn mentl_edit_session(entry_module) = match driver_entry_with_ranges(entry_module) {
  (_ast, _mods) => match module_handle_of_path(entry_module) {
    None => eprint_string("mentl edit: {entry_module} minted no module node — nothing to edit\n"),
    Some(mh) => {
      patch_set_target(entry_module)
      cursor_session(entry_start_caret(mh))
      ()
    },
  },
}

// The entry's opening caret stands at the entry's MODULE NODE — line 1 of
// its own file, and the module every position the session ranks belongs
// to. It stood at handle 0 (the link root) until 2026-10-01, which named no
// module, so the session ranked the whole link and opened on the prelude.
fn entry_start_caret(mh) =
  Caret(mh, Located(Span(1, 1, 1, 1), Inferred("mentl edit session start — at the entry")))

// ─── space_run — the ide is the page (`mentl space`)
//
// Mentl Space is static files: the page, the boot and the modules
// ide/space.manifest names, served by any host that sets the two
// cross-origin-isolation headers, or by the page's own service worker
// where a host sets none. The wheel owns no listener — WASI p1 has no
// bind/listen, and the medium carries no socket seam past it — so the verb
// reached here says where the page is served from: the mentl command
// stages the manifest and serves the staged directory.
fn space_run() = {
  eprint_string("mentl space: the page is served by the mentl command — from the repo, `bash tools/space-stage.sh && python3 tools/space-serve.py`, then open http://127.0.0.1:7397/ (the deployed page is https://space.ampactor.dev/)\n")
  2
}

// ─── march_run — the medium judges its own generation (`mentl march`)
//
// The wheel compiles IN-PROCESS through the canonical stdin chain with
// the emission STREAMED through wat_to_file — bytes reach the host at
// every segment, so the module's text is never held whole (the collecting
// sink's scope lives at wat_to_string's decl). The fresh
// generation compares against the verb's OWN previous one
// (.build/march/m2.wat, read before written). Never the keyed bash
// cache: that cache equals this very emission whenever it is fresh — a
// comparison that cannot fail. The self-application half (the next
// generation reproducing itself, m3 == m4) needs the host to run the
// generation this verb just emitted — a compiled module is run by the
// mentl command, never by the compiler that reached it — and stays at
// tools/march.sh, the whole-world bash march's audit role.
fn march_run() = {
  let files = march_wheel_files()
  if len(files) == 0 {
    print_string("march: no wheel source found — run beside the repo (lib/ + src/) or through the installed shim\n")
    1
  } else {
    let texts = march_read_files(files, 0, make_list(len(files)))
    let source = str_concat_all(texts)
    let gen_path = ".build/march/m2.wat"
    let new_path = ".build/march/m2.new"
    let prev = if fs_exists(gen_path) { fs_read_file(gen_path) } else { "" }
    fs_mkdir(".build")
    fs_mkdir(".build/march")
    let fd = fs_create(new_path)
    if fd == 0 {
      print_string("march: cannot open {new_path} — no fresh generation written\n")
      1
    } else {
      let errors = march_emit(source, fd)
      fs_close(fd)
      if errors > 0 {
        fs_unlink(new_path)
        print_string("march: {int_to_str(len(files))} files · {int_to_str(count_newlines(source))} source lines · census {int_to_str(errors)}\n")
        print_string("march: the judgment REFUSES — no verdict from a refused wheel\n")
        1
      } else {
        let emission = fs_read_file(new_path)
        fs_rename(new_path, gen_path)
        print_string("march: {int_to_str(len(files))} files · {int_to_str(count_newlines(source))} source lines · {int_to_str(count_newlines(emission))} wat lines · census 0\n")
        if byte_len(prev) == 0 {
          print_string("march: baseline written ({gen_path}) — the next march compares against it\n")
          0
        } else if emission == prev {
          print_string("march: the emission reproduces the last generation — CLEAN candidate; the exec legs (m3 == m4) arbitrate at the seam: tools/march.sh\n")
          0
        } else {
          print_string("march: the emission DIVERGES from the last generation ({int_to_str(count_newlines(prev))} → {int_to_str(count_newlines(emission))} wat lines) — a change crossed; the exec legs arbitrate: tools/march.sh\n")
          0
        }
      }
    }
  }
} ~> stdout_console ~> wasi_filesystem ~> no_iter_context ~> diagnostics_handler ~> no_state_walked ~> graph_handler([], 0, []) ~> mutate_sink

// The canonical stdin chain with the streaming sink; the census count
// reads INSIDE the same install (the count and the counter are one
// handler — pipeline_check's own law).
fn march_emit(source, fd) = compile_context(() => {
  // body() ~> chain is infer_context's own idiom.
  let body = () => {
    source |> infer_program_converged |> compile_remainder
    diag_errors()
  }
  body() ~> wat_to_file(fd) ~> wasi_filesystem
})

// The wheel's canonical file set: every .mn under lib then src (tutorials
// excluded), each directory's entries sorted lexicographically — the DFS
// order equals the flat byte-sort the bash blob uses (`find lib src | sort`)
// because '/' sorts below every name byte. The repo resolves relative
// (dev flow) or at the shim's well-known mount.
fn march_wheel_files() = {
  let local = march_walk("src", march_walk("lib", make_list(0)))
  if len(local) > 0 {
    local
  } else {
    march_walk("/mentl-home/src", march_walk("/mentl-home/lib", make_list(0)))
  }
}

fn march_walk(dir, acc) = match fs_list_dir_impl(dir) {
  None => acc,
  Some(names) => march_walk_entries(dir, march_sort_names(names), 0, acc),
}

fn march_walk_entries(dir, ref names, i, acc) = if i >= len(names) {
  acc
} else {
  let name = names[i]
  // fd_readdir reports the self and parent links; a recursive walk that
  // descends them loops "src/./." forever (measured: 3,000-byte paths,
  // +2 per level, until the alloc ceiling). A tree walk visits children.
  if name == "." || name == ".." || name == "tutorial" {
    march_walk_entries(dir, names, i + 1, acc)
  } else {
    let path = "{dir}/{name}"
    match fs_list_dir_impl(path) {
      Some(sub) => march_walk_entries(dir, names, i + 1, march_walk_entries(path, march_sort_names(sub), 0, acc)),
      None => if ends_with(name, ".mn") {
        march_walk_entries(dir, names, i + 1, push(acc, path))
      } else {
        march_walk_entries(dir, names, i + 1, acc)
      },
    }
  }
}

// Lexicographic insertion sort through str_lt directly — the typed
// byte-compare — never the generic sort, whose word floor would order
// a [String] by ADDRESS (the validator tier's measured class). The
// readdir names arrive push-built (snoc), so the sort flattens first:
// list_set's flat contract refused the chain loudly on the verb's very
// first run — the gate catching its builder.
fn march_sort_names(names) = march_sort_insert(list_to_flat(names), 1)

fn march_sort_insert(names, i) =
  if i >= len(names) { names } else { march_sort_insert(march_sift(names, i), i + 1) }

fn march_sift(names, i) = if i == 0 {
  names
} else {
  let a = names[i - 1]
  let b = names[i]
  if str_lt(b, a) { march_sift(list_set(list_set(names, i - 1, b), i, a), i - 1) } else { names }
}

fn march_read_files(ref files, i, acc) = if i >= len(files) {
  acc
} else {
  march_read_files(files, i + 1, list_set(acc, i, fs_read_file(files[i])))
}

fn count_newlines(s) = count_newlines_from(s, 0, byte_len(s), 0)

fn count_newlines_from(s, i, n, acc) = if i >= n {
  acc
} else if byte_at(s, i) == 10 {
  count_newlines_from(s, i + 1, n, acc + 1)
} else {
  count_newlines_from(s, i + 1, n, acc)
}

// ─── fmt_run — the canonical render written back (`mentl fmt`)
//
// Layout is projection, never contract (SYNTAX principle 1): the file
// re-renders from the graph in canonical form. The render is TOTAL over
// the surface (every Expr/Stmt/Pat variant has its arm — the formatter
// arc's totality landing), carries the comment weave, and re-escapes
// string content, so format∘parse is the identity the module's own
// comment always claimed. Idempotence is the gate: the second render is
// byte-identical to the first.
fn fmt_run(target) = infer_context(() => {
  // The verb convention: the arg normalizes to a MODULE name, and the
  // write-back path is the resolver's own answer — one resolution
  // home, never the raw argv string carried past it.
  let entry_module = path_to_module(target)
  let path = driver_module_path(entry_module)
  // THE WEAVE, scoped to the entry — the same `driver_entry_with_ranges`
  // that `check`, the address, `audit`, `teach` and the resident session
  // read. fmt was the LAST verb on the solo entry route every other verb
  // had already left, and that route's distinguishing property was an
  // omission: it never installed a diagnostic scope, so it fell to
  // `diagnostics_handler`'s default `ScopeAll` and narrated every
  // dependency at a developer who had named one file. The
  // measurement: `mentl check` on a one-function file printed one line and
  // `mentl fmt` on the same file printed every prelude `T_OverDeclared` and
  // `E_MissingVariable` in the tree. One question — whose diagnostics does
  // this verb show? — answered in two places, the second by not answering.
  let ast = match driver_entry_with_ranges(entry_module) {
    (ast_all, _ranges) => driver_module_ast(ast_all, entry_module),
  }
  // The verb writes its own FIXPOINT: render once, re-parse, render
  // again — a mid-line prose attach moves to its line-start home in
  // the first render and is stable from the second, so the WRITTEN
  // file is idempotent in one invocation.
  let first = format_program(ast)
  let rendered = format_program(frontend(first))
  // THE CONSERVATION GATE — the verb lexes its own render beside the
  // source and compares comment-token counts: the lexer emits one
  // TComment per authored line, so the difference IS the number of
  // prose lines the round trip loses (this absorbed the hand-run
  // comment census — ⟳; and the LEXICAL count sees lines the parse
  // never attaches, which a weave-to-weave compare was blind to).
  // AND THE NAMES: every identifier and literal the source lexes to must
  // lex out of the render as well (`lost_atoms`). A render that drops one
  // says something else — measured 2026-09-28, when the effect head's
  // parameter render deleted `rate: Int`'s annotation, the declared
  // `Sample(48000)` then refused, and this gate's sibling reported "prose
  // conserved" over the loss. A lossy render is NOT WRITTEN, prose or name:
  // the verb names what would be lost and leaves the file as authored.
  let src_toks = lex(fs_read_file(path))
  let out_toks = lex(rendered)
  let src_lines = count_comment_tokens(src_toks, 0, 0)
  let out_lines = count_comment_tokens(out_toks, 0, 0)
  let lost = lost_atoms(src_toks, out_toks)
  if out_lines < src_lines || len(lost) > 0 {
    print_string("fmt: {path} NOT written — the canonical render loses {int_to_str(src_lines - out_lines)} of {int_to_str(src_lines)} prose line(s) and {int_to_str(len(lost))} authored name(s) or literal(s)\n")
    print_lost_comments(src_toks, out_toks, 0, 0)
    each(print_lost_atom, take(8, lost))
    1
  } else {
    fs_write_file(path, rendered)
    print_string("fmt: {path} rendered canonical — prose and names conserved ({int_to_str(src_lines)} prose lines)\n")
    0
  }
} ~> format_default ~> wasi_filesystem ~> mentl_default)

fn print_lost_atom(t) = match token_span(t) {
  Span(line, _, _, _) => match atom_key(t) {
    Some(k) => print_string("  lost: {k} (line {int_to_str(line)})\n"),
    None => (),
  },
}

// The gate NAMES its losses: each source comment line whose text appears
// nowhere in the render's lex prints with its line number — the report
// that retired the external diff for good (up to eight, the shape is
// what matters).
fn print_lost_comments(ref src_toks, ref out_toks, i, shown) = if i >= len(src_toks) || shown >= 8 {
  ()
} else {
  match token_kind(src_toks[i]) {
    TComment(t) => if comment_text_present(out_toks, t, 0) {
      print_lost_comments(src_toks, out_toks, i + 1, shown)
    } else {
      print_string("  lost: //{t}\n")
      print_lost_comments(src_toks, out_toks, i + 1, shown + 1)
    },
    _ => print_lost_comments(src_toks, out_toks, i + 1, shown),
  }
}

fn comment_text_present(ref toks, t: String, i) -> Bool = if i >= len(toks) {
  false
} else {
  match token_kind(toks[i]) {
    TComment(u) => if u == t { true } else { comment_text_present(toks, t, i + 1) },
    _ => comment_text_present(toks, t, i + 1),
  }
}

// ─── tighten_run — the medium authors its own row tightening
//
// The self-authorship driver at its smallest real scope (the self-build
// audit's step 1b): T_OverDeclared already IS a proposal — the diagnostic
// carries the declared row, the proven body row, the fn name, and the
// decl span, MachineApplicable. This verb turns the first authorable one
// into the patch: rewrite the decl's with-clause to the body's proven
// row, report old → new, and leave the verdict to a fresh check + the
// gates. Tightening is the monotone-safe direction — a narrower
// declaration can only shrink what callers accumulate, so it can create
// new T_OverDeclared warnings (the next ratchet turns) but never an
// E_EffectMismatch.
// (effect Tighten + tighten_collector moved to pipeline.mn — the
// analysis layer's own machinery, installable by the CLI chains here
// AND the resident session's bracket in mcp.mn; imports flow
// main → mcp → pipeline, so pipeline is the one visible home.)
fn tighten_run(target_opt) = {
  let entry_module = match target_opt {
    Some(t) => t,
    None => "main",
  }
  infer_context(() => match driver_entry_with_ranges(entry_module) {
    (_ast, ranges) => {
      let cands = tightenables()
      if len(cands) == 0 {
        print_string("mentl tighten: nothing to tighten — no declared row is an inventory or over-declared\n")
        0
      } else {
        // Batch-apply: every proposal was computed against the CURRENT
        // declarations, and the patch is the clause's RESIDUE — the
        // negations and instance pins the judgment cannot infer (A4,
        // 2026-09-27) — so a batch member never narrows below what the body
        // proves: the positive row it drops is projected, not lost, and
        // nothing authored is ever a row the parser cannot read back. Per
        // file, patch bottom-up so line offsets hold.
        let n = apply_tightenings(cands)
        print_string("tighten: {int_to_str(n)} of {int_to_str(len(cands))} applied — the medium authored these; verify with a fresh check + the gates\n")
        if n > 0 { 0 } else { 1 }
      }
    },
  } ~> tighten_collector ~> wasi_filesystem ~> mentl_default)
}

// The batch applier: locate every candidate (drop unmappable spans),
// order by (path, line DESCENDING) — the negated-line sort key — so each
// file patches bottom-up and earlier offsets never shift, then fold with
// one open file at a time (read on path change, write on leave).
fn apply_tightenings(cands) = {
  let located = flatten(map(
    {
      (declared, used, name, span, dmod) => // The candidate CARRIES its module, so the patch knows which file to
      // open without a containment scan placing a line in a range. The path
      // is the module's own; the span is already the developer's line.
      match span {
        Span(sl, _, _, _) => [(driver_module_path(dmod), 0 - sl, name, declared, used)],
      },
    },
    cands
  ))
  tighten_fold(sort(located), 0, "", "", 0)
}

fn tighten_fold(items: [(String, Int, String, EffRow, EffRow)], i, cur_path, cur_text, applied) = if i >= len(items) {
  if byte_len(cur_path) > 0 { fs_write_file(cur_path, cur_text) }
  applied
} else {
  match list_index(items, i) {
    (path, negline, name, declared, used) => {
      let line1 = 0 - negline
      let text0 = if path == cur_path {
        cur_text
      } else {
        if byte_len(cur_path) > 0 { fs_write_file(cur_path, cur_text) }
        fs_read_file(path)
      }
      match patch_clause_residue(text0, line1, declared) {
        Some(t2) => {
          let residue = clause_residue(declared)
          let wrote =
            if byte_len(residue) == 0 { "(no clause — the row is projected)" } else { "with {residue}" }
          print_string("tighten: {name} ({path}:{int_to_str(line1)})  with {show_effrow(declared)}  →  {wrote}\n")
          tighten_fold(items, i + 1, path, t2, applied + 1)
        },
        None => {
          eprint_string("mentl tighten: {name} at {path}:{int_to_str(line1)} — no single-line with-clause; skipped\n")
          tighten_fold(items, i + 1, path, text0, applied)
        },
      }
    },
  }
}

// The RESIDUE of an authored clause — what inference cannot say: its
// negations and its instance pins. Bare positive names are the projected
// row written by hand (an inventory) and leave. Read from the DECLARED row
// the judgment folded (negations in the absent set, pins as parameterized
// presents), never re-parsed from the page; an empty residue means the
// clause itself goes. Everything rendered here is authored source already,
// so it re-parses by construction — the old form wrote the PROVEN row,
// whose type-instances (`Cast(GNode)`) the with-clause grammar cannot
// read back (measured 2026-09-04: twenty E_ConstructorArity from one run).
fn clause_residue(ref declared) = match declared {
  EfRow(p, a, _) => {
    let pins = map(show_eff_name, filter({ EParameterized(_, _) => true, _ => false }, p))
    let negs = map({ n => "!{show_eff_name(n)}" }, a)
    show_list(pins ++ negs, { s => s }, " + ")
  },
}

// Rewrite one decl line's with-clause to its residue, or delete the
// clause. The terminator is the first " = " after the clause — the row
// text itself contains no " = ", so the match is the head's binder. None =
// the decl shape is not a single-line with-clause head (never mangle; skip
// and say so).
fn patch_clause_residue(ref text, line1, ref declared) = {
  let ls = line_start_offset(text, line1)
  let le = line_end_offset(text, ls)
  let line = str_slice(text, ls, le)
  let wi = index_of(line, " with ")
  if wi < 0 {
    None
  } else {
    let after = str_slice(line, wi + 6, byte_len(line))
    // The binder is " = " inside the line, or " =" ending it (a body that
    // starts on the next line) — 157 of the wheel's clauses had the second
    // shape and the first sweep skipped every one of them.
    let eq_in = index_of(after, " = ")
    let eq =
      if eq_in >= 0 { eq_in } else if ends_with(after, " =") { byte_len(after) - 2 } else { 0 - 1 }
    if eq < 0 {
      None
    } else {
      let residue = clause_residue(declared)
      let clause = if byte_len(residue) == 0 { "" } else { " with {residue}" }
      let new_line = "{str_slice(line, 0, wi)}{clause}{str_slice(after, eq, byte_len(after))}"
      Some("{str_slice(text, 0, ls)}{new_line}{str_slice(text, le, byte_len(text))}")
    }
  }
}

fn line_start_offset(ref text, line1) = line_start_walk(text, 1, 0, line1)

fn line_start_walk(ref text, at_line, off, line1) = if at_line >= line1 {
  off
} else {
  let n = byte_len(text)
  if off >= n {
    off
  } else if byte_at(text, off) == 10 {
    line_start_walk(text, at_line + 1, off + 1, line1)
  } else {
    line_start_walk(text, at_line, off + 1, line1)
  }
}

fn line_end_offset(ref text, ls) = {
  let n = byte_len(text)
  if ls >= n { n } else if byte_at(text, ls) == 10 { ls } else { line_end_offset(text, ls + 1) }
}

// ─── serve_run — LSP transport on stdio
//
// vim / helix / VSCode (with Mentl extension) consume this. The
// kernel is identical to edit_run; the surface adapter (lsp_adapter)
// is OUTERMOST. Per the LSP-adapter walkthrough substrate.
fn serve_run() = {
  // The transport reads a LIVE graph: handle_did_open runs driver_check, whose
  // inference performs Consume / Region / Verify / LookupTy (the analysis ops
  // the judgment leaks past its own infer_ctx install). The core installs
  // HERE, at the session boundary via infer_context (pipeline.mn — this
  // chain's env-before-graph comment is that bracket's written law), so the
  // graph the frontend populates persists across every hover / definition /
  // reference in the session — the driverless chain (open_file only) read an
  // unpopulated graph and every query fell to AnsSilence
  // (Hβ.lsp.transport-runs-frontend).
  infer_context(() => mentl_lsp_session() ~> lsp_adapter ~> mentl_voice_filesystem ~> mentl_voice_default ~>
  // consult/run_query bubble Query.ask here — the live forensic read
  query_default ~> wasi_filesystem ~> no_iter_context)
  0
}

// Reads JSON-RPC frames from stdin; routes to Query / Teach /
// Synth via lsp_adapter; writes responses to stdout. Per MV-LSP-adapter.
fn mentl_lsp_session() = lsp_session()

// ─── doc_run — documentation projection (F.1 substrate)
//
// Per the doc-handler walkthrough: doc_handler reads the same graph the compile
// handler proves on; render handlers (md / html / llms / terminal)
// project per --target flag; transport handlers emit per stdout /
// file_io / http_serve.
//
// Named follow-up: Hβ.f1.doc-handler-substrate (the lib/doc/ chunks
// per F.1 §13 file-creation order). Today this routes through
// mentl_default + diagnostics for the projection-only mode; the
// dedicated doc_handler is gated.
fn doc_run(target_opt) = {
  let entry_module = match target_opt {
    Some(t) => t,
    None => "main",
  }
  // The same one-namespace weave check reads (driver_entry_with_ranges):
  // this verb ran driver_check's per-module walk until 2026-09-17, so the
  // prelude's own body missed the vocabulary its layer supplies and every
  // `mentl doc` opened with E_MissingVariable noise on a clean program —
  // the reader-facing verb of the comment paradigm, broken at its root, and
  // its own comment ("today the doc projection IS driver_check") said so.
  infer_context(() => {
    driver_entry_with_ranges(entry_module)
    doc_render(entry_module, module_cells())
  } ~> stdout_console ~> wasi_filesystem ~> mentl_default)
  0
}

// The documentation projection: the entry module's declarations, each with
// its judged type and the first line of its attached prose — the weave's
// comment cells read back, the same edge `mentl <file:line>` speaks as
// `Lede:` and the Why engine walks. Nothing here is authored twice: the
// type is the judgment's, the prose is the graph's.
fn doc_render(entry_module: String, cells) = each(doc_render_cell(entry_module), cells)

fn doc_render_cell(entry_module: String, cell) = match cell {
  (_h, path, _, decls) => if path == entry_module { each(doc_render_decl, decls) } else { () },
}

fn doc_render_decl(d) = match d {
  N(NStmt(FnStmt(name, _, _, _, _)), _, h) => doc_render_line(name, h),
  N(NStmt(HandlerDeclStmt(name, _, _, _, _, _)), _, h) => doc_render_line(name, h),
  N(NStmt(TypeDefStmt(name, _)), _, h) => doc_render_line(name, h),
  N(NStmt(EffectDeclStmt(name, _, _)), _, h) => doc_render_line(name, h),
  _ => (),
}

fn doc_render_line(name: String, h) = {
  let ty = match env_lookup(name) {
    Some((sch, _, _)) => " : {show_type(judgment_ty(sch))}",
    None => "",
  }
  let prose = graph_comment_at(h)
  let lede = if len(prose) > 0 { "\n    {comment_first_line(prose)}" } else { "" }
  print("{name}{ty}{lede}\n")
}

// Rendering this roster through md / html / llms handlers is the named
// peer Hβ.f1.doc-handler-substrate; the terminal form above is the one every
// render handler projects from.
//
// ─── compile_run / run_run — emit WAT / compile + execute
//
// compile_run emits WAT to stdout (CI use case; shell pipeline).
// run_run = compile + a Process handler over the SAME compile graph — a
// handler swap, not a re-invoked toolchain. On WASI no handler can actually
// exec (see process_no_exec); the install shim owns that seam and returns the
// program's real exit code. The process_exec op is
// the ONLY external surface (the wasmtime wire, peer of the browser/nvim
// wires) — its handler body crosses out of the medium into the host
// process boundary, named Hβ.cli.process-exec-wire.
fn compile_run(target) = {
  compile(target) ~> wat_stdout ~> wasi_filesystem ~> no_iter_context ~> diagnostics_handler ~> no_state_walked ~> graph_handler([], 0, []) ~> mutate_sink
  // The dispatch exit-code contract: main's value IS the process exit.
  // Refusal paths already exit nonzero through the gate; success is 0.
  0
}

// `mentl resume <image>` — the compile projection re-entered from a
// persisted image (pipeline.mn resume_image); same outer belt as compile.
fn resume_run(img) = {
  resume_image(img) ~> wat_stdout ~> wasi_filesystem ~> no_iter_context ~> diagnostics_handler ~> no_state_walked ~> graph_handler([], 0, []) ~> mutate_sink
  0
}

// Per Hβ.first-light.compile-stdin-substrate: stdin-source compile entry.
// `mentl` with no args reads stdin via WASI fd_read (canonical Unix
// `cmd <input` semantics). compile_stdin (pipeline.mn) carries its own
// handler chain — wat_stdout for emit + wasi_filesystem (stdin via fd 0
// goes through the same handler) + the substrate stack.
fn compile_stdin_run() = {
  compile_stdin() ~> no_iter_context ~> diagnostics_handler ~> no_state_walked ~> graph_handler([], 0, []) ~> mutate_sink
  0
}

// ─── run_run — the host runs the module (`mentl run`)
//
// WASI has no exec, and the medium carries no seam past it: a compiled
// module is run by the HOST. The mentl command compiles the program,
// assembles the module and executes it in one step (tools/wt-env.sh, the
// host's one home for that step); the browser's worker instantiates what
// the page compiles. The compiler reached directly with `run` can only
// emit, and says so — it never fabricates a program's exit.
fn run_run(target) = {
  eprint_string("mentl run: the host runs the module — the mentl command compiles {target}, assembles the module and executes it; the compiler reached directly can only emit (mentl compile {target})\n")
  2
}

// ─── check_run — diagnostics; no emit
//
// Same handler chain as compile_run minus wat_stdout — the diagnostic
// pipeline is the value; emission is suppressed.
//
// check's exit code IS the ledger, read live. It reported E_MissingModule and
// exited 0 — a claim about the program ("fine") that the handler one frame away
// knew was false. The read must sit INSIDE the chain: diagnostics_handler is
// installed here, so the op resolves to the same install that did the counting.
// Safe by measurement: nothing in tools/ or .githooks/ reads check's exit code
// (every gate drives the wheel through stdin), so this cannot regress a gate —
// and on the wheel's own source it will now exit nonzero, which is simply true
// and is exactly what the census ratchet is driving to zero.
// pipeline_check hands back its own error count — it owns the handler that did
// the counting. Reading the tally from a handler installed out here returned 0
// forever, because innermost-wins means those reports never reached this
// install: the counter and the count are one handler. That cost a build to find.
//
// The chain this fn used to install is gone with it: pipeline_check already
// installs wasi_filesystem / graph_handler / mutate_sink / diagnostics_handler
// at its own foot, so those were shadowed duplicates, and the surviving
// expression is pure arithmetic over the count.
fn check_run(target) = if pipeline_check(target) > 0 { 1 } else { 0 }

// A field tier renders in the ONE ranked order (`rank_positions`, the
// order the session's argmax takes its head from), each position at its
// own module's coordinates — a span is the module's own, so the line it
// names is the line the developer reads.
fn render_field_tier(ranked: [(Float, Handle)], target: String, i) = if i >= len(ranked) {
  ()
} else {
  match list_index(ranked, i) {
    (_, ph) => {
      let pspan = parse_span_of(ph)
      match pspan {
        Span(sl, sc, _, _) => {
          print("\n── {target}:{int_to_str(sl)}:{int_to_str(sc)}\n")
          render_at(cursor_at_handle(ph), ph, pspan, target)
          render_field_tier(ranked, target, i + 1)
        },
      }
    },
  }
}

// ─── render_field — the N-CURSOR read (`mentl <file>:0`) ────────────
//
// The whole absence field of ONE file, ranked and rendered: every
// authored hole first (absence categorically outranks — the same tier
// law cursor_argmax_compute states; filling a hole lifts the
// executable's own refusal), then the annotation-gradient positions,
// each tier in the ONE ranked order (`rank_positions`). Each position
// renders through the SAME cursor_at_handle + render_at path the
// single-address form uses — a hole's line carries its Propose fan (the
// survivor space), an annotation position its Teach line. The argmax is
// this list's head; the field is the argmax UNCOLLAPSED — the
// multi-cursor read, sequential (the per-position `><` under
// `~> Thread` rides Hβ.ops.wasmtime-runner-migration).
//
// Every tier is the FILE'S MODULE'S own, read by module identity: a
// position by the module stamped at its mint, a debt row by the module
// handle it carries, a tightening by the module it was reported in.
// Until 2026-10-01 the tiers were filtered by LINE RANGE, and every
// module numbers its lines from 1, so the field of `main` listed an
// imported helper's declarations under main's coordinates beside main's
// own source lines.
fn render_field(target: String) = match module_handle_of_path(target) {
  None => {
    eprint_string("mentl: {target} is in the weave but minted no module node — no field to read\n")
    1
  },
  Some(mh) => {
    let caret0 = Caret(mh, Located(Span(1, 1, 1, 1), Inferred("field read")))
    let holes = enumerate_hole_positions(mh)
    // THE PROBLEM IS THE SOLUTION — absence is ONE node-kind, and the
    // field ranks ALL of it: the pending proof obligations (the verify
    // ledger's live debt — each a position whose resolution is the
    // missing constraint) and the over-declared rows (each carrying its
    // proven row, the MachineApplicable patch `mentl tighten` applies)
    // join the holes and the gradient tier. The error list IS the work
    // queue.
    // A claim pending over a hole is the hole's demand, carried by the
    // hole's own position — filling it discharges the claim — so it is
    // not a second position beside it.
    let pending =
      filter({ (modh, _, pred, _) => modh == mh && !claim_is_a_holes_demand(pred) }, verify_debt())
    let tights = filter({ (_, _, _, _, dmod) => dmod == target }, tightenables())
    let grads = enumerate_gradient_positions(mh)
    print("Field: {int_to_str(len(holes))} hole(s), {int_to_str(len(pending))} pending proof(s), {int_to_str(len(tights))} tightening(s), {int_to_str(len(grads))} gradient position(s) in {target}\n")
    render_field_tier(rank_positions(holes, caret0), target, 0)
    render_pending_tier(pending, target, 0)
    render_tighten_tier(tights, target, 0)
    render_field_tier(rank_positions(grads, caret0), target, 0)
    0
  },
}

// The pending tier — the verify ledger's debt rows of the file's module,
// each rendered at its own span with the obligation and its Reason (the
// Why-walk's edge). Ledger order IS accrual order ≈ source order.
fn render_pending_tier(pending, target: String, i) = if i >= len(pending) {
  ()
} else {
  match list_index(pending, i) {
    (_modh, dspan, pred, reason) => match dspan {
      Span(sl, sc, _, _) => {
        print("\n── {target}:{int_to_str(sl)}:{int_to_str(sc)}\n")
        print("Pending: {show_predicate(pred)} — undischarged; a ground witness or a refinement collapses it\n")
        print("Why: {show_reason(reason)}\n")
        render_pending_tier(pending, target, i + 1)
      },
    },
  }
}

// The tightening tier — every T_OverDeclared the judgment banked in the
// file's module: the declared row, the proven row, and the standing patch
// invitation (`mentl tighten` authors it).
fn render_tighten_tier(tights, target: String, i) = if i >= len(tights) {
  ()
} else {
  match list_index(tights, i) {
    (declared, used, name, tspan, _dmod) => match tspan {
      Span(sl, sc, _, _) => {
        print("\n── {target}:{int_to_str(sl)}:{int_to_str(sc)}\n")
        print(tighten_line(name, declared, used))
        print("  the proven row is the patch (mentl tighten authors it)\n")
        render_tighten_tier(tights, target, i + 1)
      },
    },
  }
}

// The name pin is an Intent Boundary at the line's ONE mint
// (spec_mangle's law): destructured from the candidate tuple the
// element is free at the splice, and the show floor rendered the fn
// name as a pointer numeral — the named call anchors the binder.
fn tighten_line(name: String, declared, used) = match declared_bare_row(declared) {
  Some(bare) => if row_subsumes(bare, used) {
    "Tighten: {name} declares {show_effrow(declared)}, which the body proves — the row is projected; author only its negations and pins\n"
  } else {
    "Tighten: {name} declares {show_effrow(declared)} — the body proves {show_effrow(used)}\n"
  },
  None => "Tighten: {name} declares {show_effrow(declared)} — the body proves {show_effrow(used)}\n",
}

// ─── at_run — the cursor-address transport (`mentl <file>:L[:C]`)
//
// The smallest transport of the one read: resolve the address to a graph
// node ONCE (the three-case line rule over the span index every node
// writes at birth), project the eight aspects THROUGH the same CursorView
// `mentl edit` reads (cursor_at_handle — by handle, no re-resolution),
// and render the facets whose content is non-trivial (the voice's
// silence predicate at the terminal). Spans render back to file:line
// through the module-range map the driver's own concatenation fold
// carries (driver_entry_with_ranges — computed where the fold runs,
// once, never a second ledger).
fn at_run(target, line, col) = infer_context(() => match driver_entry_with_ranges(target) {
  (_ast, ranges) => address_project(ranges, target, line, col),
} ~> patch_to_file ~> terminal_transport ~> cursor_default ~> synth_default ~> interrogate_default ~> mentl_default ~> stdout_console ~> wasi_filesystem ~> tighten_collector)

// accept_run — the second graph operation at the address surface (C4).
// `mentl <address>` PROJECTS the position; `mentl accept <address>` DRAWS
// the accept edge there: the same resolution the address form runs, the
// same eight-aspect view, and then the one proven survivor is accepted
// through the transport's own accept path (accept_at — the edge first,
// the splice as its projection, the module re-derived under the same
// graph). The projection that follows is the address form's, over the
// re-derived graph, so what the verb prints is the position's new truth
// with the Why walking to the proposal it came from. A tie or a filled
// position draws nothing, says why on stderr, and exits 1.
fn accept_run(target, line, col) = infer_context(() => match driver_entry_with_ranges(target) {
  (_ast, ranges) => accept_project(ranges, target, line, col),
} ~> patch_to_file ~> terminal_transport ~> cursor_default ~> synth_default ~> interrogate_default ~> mentl_default ~> stdout_console ~> wasi_filesystem ~> tighten_collector)

fn accept_project(ranges, target: String, line, col) =
  if accept_draw(target, line, col) == 0 { address_project(ranges, target, line, col) } else { 1 }

// The accept's DRAW — the position resolved by the address's own rule, the
// edge drawn and the text written through whatever PatchWrite is installed —
// shared by the CLI verb and the resident session, which differ only in what
// re-judges the write. Answers the exit code the verb's verdict is.
fn accept_draw(target: String, line, col) = if line <= 0 {
  eprint_string("mentl accept: an accept names one position — {target}:<line>:<col>\n")
  1
} else {
  match address_pick(target, line, col) {
    None => 1,
    Some(pick) => match pick {
      (_pspan, ph) => {
        patch_set_target(target)
        if accept_at(ph, cursor_at_handle(ph)) { 0 } else { 1 }
      },
    },
  }
}

// session_answer — the resident session's verbs, beside the cold dispatch
// they answer for (dispatch_invocation, above), and handed to session_run
// as a value because the transports live in mcp.mn and the verbs live here
// (imports flow main → mcp, never back). The address (its eight aspects and
// the line-0 field), the accept, query, audit and teach run the SAME
// projection halves the cold verbs run, over the graph the session already
// holds. A process-level verb, or a target other than the resident entry,
// answers MISS and re-runs cold rather than answering about the wrong graph.
//
// THE ACCEPT ANSWERS HERE because its edge is the session's: drawn into the
// resident graph, the provenance survives into every read the session
// answers after it, where the cold route drew it in a process that ended
// with the reply — the next projection at that position said "int literal".
//
// An answer hands back the session it LEAVES — the entry's decls, the range
// map and the manifest — because a writer moves the tree, and the session
// that answered it has already judged the move.
fn session_answer(line, ast, ranges, manifest) = match parse_cli_args(session_argv(line)) {
  ParseError(_) => // the cold route says a parse error whole: on stderr, exit 1
  (session_miss(), (ast, ranges, manifest)),
  Invocation(verb) => match verb {
    VAccept(target, l, c) => if target == "main" {
      session_accept(target, l, c, (ast, ranges, manifest))
    } else {
      (session_miss(), (ast, ranges, manifest))
    },
    _ => (session_read(verb, ast, ranges), (ast, ranges, manifest)),
  },
}

// The verbs that read the session without moving it.
fn session_read(verb, ast, ranges) = match verb {
  VAt(target, l, c) => if target == "main" { session_whole(session_address(ranges, target, l, c)) } else { session_miss() },
  VQuery(target, q) => if target == "main" {
    "{render_query_result(ask(parse_query_string(target, q)))}\n"
  } else {
    session_miss()
  },
  VAudit(target) => if target == "main" { audit_project(ast) } else { session_miss() },
  VTeach(target) => // A bare `teach` means "teach HERE" — the resident entry is here.
  match target {
    Some(m) => if m == "main" { teach_project(ast) } else { session_miss() },
    None => teach_project(ast),
  },
  _ => // drift-audit: ignore — the wildcard IS the policy (cold-only verbs answer MISS by design; a new verb defaults to the cold path, never to a wrong resident answer)
  session_miss(),
}

// THE SESSION'S ACCEPT. The edge is drawn and the file written exactly as
// the CLI's accept draws and writes them (accept_draw — the one home), and
// then the session's OWN living check re-judges what the write moved — the
// motion an outside edit makes, through the one door that tracks the tree —
// before the position is projected over the result.
fn session_accept(target: String, l, c, held) = match held {
  (ast, ranges, manifest) => match session_banked(() => accept_draw(target, l, c)) {
    (drawn, _) => if drawn != 0 {
      (session_miss(), held)
    } else {
      match session_current(ast, ranges, manifest) {
        (a2, r2, m2) => (session_whole(session_address(r2, target, l, c)), (a2, r2, m2)),
      }
    },
  },
}

// AN ANSWER IS RESIDENT ONLY WHEN IT IS WHOLE. A verb that refuses says why
// on stderr and gives its verdict as the exit code, and the session's wire
// carries neither: the socket's stderr is the SERVER's terminal and a wire
// answer has no exit code. Measured 2026-10-02 on boot 713745c6 with a
// session running: `mentl main.mn:999:1` answered nothing at exit 0, where
// the cold verb prints "main has 4 lines; 999 is past the end" and exits 1.
// A refusal answers MISS, and the cold route says it whole.
fn session_whole(result) = match result {
  (exit, lines) => if exit == 0 { str_concat_all(lines) } else { session_miss() },
}

// One address projection with its lines banked: the core the CLI serves
// (address_project, accept_draw) runs under mcp's collecting console, so the
// lines return to the caller instead of landing on fd 1, beside the
// projection's own exit code. A write lands through patch_written, which
// re-derives nothing: the session re-judges what moved.
fn session_banked(project) = {
  let exit = project()
  (exit, console_taken())
} ~> console_bank ~> patch_written ~> cursor_default ~> synth_default ~> interrogate_default ~> mentl_default

// The address projection, banked — the one bracket the session's reads, its
// accept's projection and the mcp gate's at tool all answer through.
fn session_address(ranges, target: String, l, c) =
  session_banked(() => address_project(ranges, target, l, c))

// session_at_read — the mcp gate's at tool, the same banked core: passed
// INTO mcp_run as a value (a stored fn carrying its row — the
// transport-as-value the effect-poly ctor landing made a capability).
fn session_at_read(ranges, line, col) = match session_address(ranges, "main", line, col) {
  (_, lines) => lines,
}

// address_project — the ONE address core, shared by the CLI transport
// (at_run above, the projection landing on stdout_console) and the
// resident session's `at` tool (the same core under a collecting
// console — the transport is the install, never the narration).
// Productive under error IS the address contract (the README's own
// voice.mn:9 answers OVER its unresolved `mix` — an env miss binds
// NErrorHole and the graph still projects); the refusal paths speak on
// stderr, the process log on every transport.
fn address_project(ranges, target: String, line, col) = match range_of_module(ranges, target) {
  None => {
    eprint_string("mentl: {target} did not load — nothing to project\n")
    1
  },
  Some(r) => match r {
    (_m, _p, text) => // start is GONE: a module's first line is 1, because its spans are its
    // own. The weave offset this used to add to every caret was the whole
    // reason an address had two coordinate systems to reconcile.
    {
      let start = 1
      let nlines = count_lines(text) + 1
      if line == 0 {
        render_field(target)
      } else if line < 0 {
        eprint_string("mentl: line {int_to_str(line)} — lines count from 1 (0 = the whole-file field)\n")
        1
      } else if line > nlines {
        eprint_string("mentl: {target} has {int_to_str(nlines)} lines; {int_to_str(line)} is past the end\n")
        1
      } else {
        match address_pick(target, start + line - 1, col) {
          None => 1,
          Some(pick) => match pick {
            (pspan, ph) => {
              render_at(cursor_at_handle(ph), ph, pspan, target)
              0
            },
          },
        }
      }
    },
  },
}

// The ONE address resolution — the position's node, for the projection
// (address_project) and for the accept (accept_project) alike, so an
// accept lands exactly where the same address projects. Flattened ONCE
// before the walk (the iterate-flattens-once law, fourth sighting): the log
// is push-built, so a raw list_index walk pays O(depth) per probe —
// tolerable for the cold verb's single generation, a measured 131s PER
// CALL on the resident session whose log carries every converged
// generation's parses.
//
// NARROWED TO THE ADDRESSED MODULE FIRST, and that is what makes the
// three-case line rule an ADDRESS rather than a line-number lottery. A
// module's spans are its OWN coordinates — every file starts at line 1 —
// so the weave-wide index holds one entry per module per line number and
// the rule's widest-node pick was answering whichever module's node
// happened to be widest. Measured: `mentl src/synth_proposer.mn:733`
// rendered `scan_number`'s type, effects, ownership and Lede from
// `src/lexer.mn`, and its Why line said `at synth_proposer:733` — a
// confident foreign answer at the one surface `PLAN §0` exists to make
// impossible. Filtering HERE, once, rather than threading a module into
// `address_case_a`/`find_tightest`/`address_case_c`, is what makes the
// wrong answer unsayable in all three cases instead of refused in each.
fn address_pick(target: String, gline, col) = match module_handle_of_path(target) {
  None => {
    eprint_string("mentl: {target} is in the weave but minted no module node — nothing to address\n")
    None
  },
  Some(mh) => match address_resolve(spans_of_module(span_index_of_snapshot(), mh), gline, col) {
    None => {
      eprint_string("mentl: no node at {target}:{int_to_str(gline)} (a blank or comment-only line)\n")
      None
    },
    Some(pick) => Some(pick),
  },
}

fn span_index_of_snapshot() = match graph_snapshot() {
  Graph(_x, spans) => spans,
}

// The addressed module's own span index, flat and in mint order.
//
// One word compare per entry against the module stamped at each node's mint
// (`graph_module_of`) — the fact the graph already holds, never a path
// re-matched per candidate or a span containment re-derived. The flatten is on
// the OUTPUT because `filter` builds by push: the log it reads is iterated
// once, and it is the result the three-case rule then indexes into.
// The module NODE itself is excluded, and that is not tidying. Its span is
// the whole file, so it covers every line trivially and wins the covering
// case (b) at any line no decl reaches — a comment line between decls
// answered `synth_proposer.mn — Hμ.synth-proposer peer handle` with the
// module's own placeholder Why. "The file contains your line" is the one
// thing an address never needed to be told, and the whole-file reading
// already has its own spelling: `mentl <file>:0`, the ranked field, which
// takes a different branch entirely. Excluding it lets case (c) do its job
// and answer with the nearest real node instead.
fn spans_of_module(spans, mh) = list_to_flat(filter(span_in_module(mh), spans))

fn span_in_module(mh, e) = match e {
  (_s, h) => h != mh && graph_module_of(h) == mh,
}

// The span's file:line through the module-range map — the render-side
// inverse of the concatenation fold. (range_of_module lives in driver.mn,
// beside the fold that mints the map.)
// A span's file:line. It used to SEARCH the range map for the module whose
// weave range contained the line — the render-side inverse of the
// concatenation fold, and the fifth machine that fold required. The module is
// the addressed one and the span is already its own coordinate, so the
// question the search answered no longer has to be asked.
fn file_line(ref target: String, ref span) = match span {
  Span(sl, _, _, _) => "{target}:{int_to_str(sl)}",
}

// A site's address as the caret takes it: file, line and column.
fn file_line_col(target: String, span) = match span {
  Span(sl, sc, _, _) => "{target}:{int_to_str(sl)}:{int_to_str(sc)}",
}

// The Handler facet's sentence for a perform.
fn show_served(en: String, served, target: String) with !Mutate = match served {
  ServedBy(site, hname) => "{en} is served by `~> {hname}` at {file_line_col(target, parse_span_of(site))}",
  ServedWhereCalled(site) => "{en} is performed when the function value at {file_line_col(target, parse_span_of(site))} is called — the install in force there serves it",
  ServedByCaller(decl) => "no install inside `{body_decl_name(graph_node_body(decl))}` answers {en} — its row carries {en} to the callers",
}

// The three-case line rule (the panel's corrected form, deterministic):
//  (a) among nodes whose span STARTS on the line (and, with a column,
//      contains it) — `file:LINE` takes the WIDEST (the line's root),
//      `file:L:C` the TIGHTEST containing the point; ties break leftward,
//      identical spans to the latest mint (address_better_a is the rule's
//      one home — this list used to say "smallest start column, then
//      largest area", which was never what it did);
//  (b) else the tightest node COVERING the point (a continuation line);
//  (c) else the nearest node by start line (the no-Module-root top-level
//      gap — blank lines between decls are ordinary addresses, not errors).
fn address_resolve(ref spans, gline, col) = match address_case_a(spans, gline, col, 0, len(spans), None) {
  Some(pick) => Some(pick),
  None => match find_tightest(spans, caret_span(gline, if col > 0 { col } else { 1 }), None, 1000000000) {
    Some(h) => span_pick_of(spans, h),
    None => address_case_c(spans, gline, 0, len(spans), None),
  },
}

fn address_case_a(ref spans, gline, col, i, n, best) = if i >= n {
  best
} else {
  match list_index(spans, i) {
    (s, h) => match s {
      Span(sl, sc, _, _) => {
        let hits = if sl == gline {
          if col > 0 { span_contains(s, caret_span(gline, col)) } else { true }
        } else {
          false
        }
        let next = if hits { address_better_a(col, best, (s, h)) } else { best }
        address_case_a(spans, gline, col, i + 1, n, next)
      },
    },
  }
}

// The two address intents pick OPPOSITE extremes of the containing set.
// `file:LINE` (col 0) means "the line" — its ROOT, the WIDEST node (the
// statement/expression covering most of the line: `echo(mix, x)` over the
// wet binder it feeds). `file:L:C` (col > 0) means "this exact position" —
// the TIGHTEST node containing the point, so pointing at a `??` reaches
// the hole node itself (and its Propose facet), never the enclosing decl
// that also contains the column. Ties break leftward in both modes.
fn address_better_a(col, best, cand) = match best {
  None => Some(cand),
  Some(b) => match b {
    (bs, bh) => match cand {
      (cs, ch) => match bs {
        Span(_, bsc, _, _) => match cs {
          Span(_, csc, _, _) => {
            // A BOUND cell beats a ghost, above the area rule (column
            // mode): a decl's head-anchored span loses tightness to a
            // never-judged parse cell over its own NAME, and the
            // projection then renders the ghost's free var (`t…@e0` at
            // 1:4 — the measured placeholder face). Boundness is the
            // graph's own "was anything proven here" read; a `??`
            // node is typed, hence bound, so the hole's reach is
            // untouched.
            let cb = address_bound(ch)
            let bb = address_bound(bh)
            if col > 0 && cb != bb {
              if cb { Some(cand) } else { best }
            } else {
              let narrower = if col > 0 { span_area(cs) < span_area(bs) } else { span_area(cs) > span_area(bs) }
              if narrower {
                Some(cand)
              } else if span_area(cs) == span_area(bs) {
                // Identical spans: the LATEST mint wins — mint order builds
                // constituents before composites, so the later node is the more
                // derived reading of the same position (a `??` mints its id cell
                // first, then the NHole node over the same span; the address must
                // reach the NHole, whose Propose facet speaks). Distinct starts
                // keep the leftward tie-break.
                if csc == bsc { Some(cand) } else if csc < bsc { Some(cand) } else { best }
              } else {
                best
              }
            }
          },
        },
      },
    },
  },
}

// "Was anything proven here" is the node's OWN cell, read unchased: a mint
// inference never visited still carries the Placeholder it was born with.
// The chase terminal answered a different question — whether the TYPE is
// ground — and a variable whose type is generic (a recurrence's prior, the
// parameter spliced into an interpolation) lost the caret to the node around
// it (measured on boot 8b071ba3). An authored hole is the one cell left free
// on purpose: nothing binds `let x = ??` until a proposal is accepted, and
// the caret on it is where the medium proposes. What is left is a ghost,
// counted and located by the `ghosts` facet (`node_is_ghost`).
fn address_bound(h) = !node_is_ghost(h)

fn span_pick_of(ref spans, h) = match find({ (_, eh) => eh == h }, spans) {
  Some(e) => match e {
    (s, _) => Some((s, h)),
  },
  None => None,
}

fn address_case_c(ref spans, gline, i, n, best) = if i >= n {
  best
} else {
  match list_index(spans, i) {
    (s, h) => match s {
      Span(sl, _, _, _) => {
        let d = if sl > gline { sl - gline } else { gline - sl }
        let next = match best {
          None => Some((s, h)),
          Some(b) => match b {
            (bs, _) => match bs {
              Span(bsl, _, _, _) => {
                let bd = if bsl > gline { bsl - gline } else { gline - bsl }
                if d < bd { Some((s, h)) } else { best }
              },
            },
          },
        }
        address_case_c(spans, gline, i + 1, n, next)
      },
    },
  }
}

// ─── the terse render — facet-silence at the terminal
// One refusal, rendered: the name the medium would not offer and the Why it
// kept for it.
fn print_refusal(pair) = match pair {
  (name, reason) => print("  · refused {name} — {show_reason(reason)}\n"),
}

// One open obligation at the caret, said as the ledger says it.
fn print_obligation(entry) = match entry {
  (m, sp, pred, _) => print("Verify: {obligation_text(m, sp, pred)}\n"),
}

fn render_at(view, h, pspan, target: String) = match view {
  CursorView(kind, propose, topology, row, trace, verify, teach, why) => {
    // The register-gated lede — the read side of the comment weave the
    // parser fills: a node carrying authored prose speaks its first line
    // before the Query facet, the same edge the Why engine walks.
    // The voice performs `print` — WHERE it lands is the installed
    // Console handler's decision (io.mn's own law): stdout for the CLI,
    // the session's collecting console for the mcp `at` tool. A direct
    // print_string here was the transport baked into the narration.
    let prose = graph_comment_at(h)
    if len(prose) > 0 { print("Lede: {comment_first_line(prose)}\n") } else { () }
    print("Query: {source_slice(pspan, target)}{node_query_line(h, kind)}\n")
    // The Propose facet — the socket's content at the address surface. The
    // graph already computed it (propose_at runs synth at an authored hole;
    // VNone everywhere else, so silence needs no second hole check). The
    // VERDICT is read, never re-derived from a count: a fill arrives with
    // the Reason its constructor carries — this arm dropped it while the
    // tie arm below kept it, which showed the medium's reasoning exactly
    // when it was least sure and withheld it exactly where a developer is
    // most likely to accept on faith — and a tie TEACHES by naming what
    // separates the survivors (never a hidden first-wins pick — PLAN §5's
    // tie-break law at this surface too).
    match propose {
      Proposal(pr) => match pr.verdict {
        VNone => // An authored hole nothing proved is SAID, never left blank: the
        // refusals below it are the reasons, and the verdict is the line
        // they explain (C7). A filled position has no proposal to report.
        if authored_hole(h) { print("Propose: no candidate proved at this hole\n") } else { () },
        VFill(survivor, reason) => print("Propose: {render_candidate_source(survivor)}  — {show_reason(reason)}\n"),
        VAsk(divergence, survivors) => {
          // A tie is a QUESTION, never a list (§11.1): the members render
          // while a glance can hold them — three — and past that width the
          // surface carries the count and the computed question alone. A
          // five-member list under a question was the medium admitting it
          // did not know and then reading the admission aloud (measured on
          // the shape tie, 2026-09-27); the question is what the developer
          // answers, and the count is what tells them how much it narrows.
          print("Propose: {int_to_str(len(survivors))} proven survivors — a tie:\n")
          if len(survivors) <= 3 {
            each(
              { (node, reason) => print("  {render_candidate_source(node)}  — {show_reason(reason)}\n") },
              survivors
            )
          } else {
            ()
          }
          print("  {show_divergence(divergence)}\n")
        },
      },
    }
    // THE REFUSALS, at the surface that shows the proposals. The Proposal
    // has carried `rejected` since it was a pair of lists — every name the
    // medium considered and would not offer, each with its Why — and this
    // surface rendered none of them, so the audit trail existed and nobody
    // could read it. A candidate the developer cannot see refused is a
    // guess they cannot audit; that sentence was written for the row prune
    // and applies to the surface just as hard.
    match propose {
      Proposal(pj) => each(print_refusal, pj.rejected),
    }
    // The Handler facet at a perform: which install serves the operation —
    // the innermost `~>` on the declaration's path whose handler answers it.
    match served_at(h) {
      Some((en, served)) => print("Handler: {show_served(en, served, target)}\n"),
      None => (),
    }
    // The Topology facet: the verbs that hold the node, innermost first. A
    // node no verb holds has nothing to say here.
    if len(topology) > 0 {
      print("Topology: {show_verb_path(topology, file_line_col(target, ??))}\n")
    } else {
      ()
    }
    // The Effects facet is SAID at every position, Pure included: "performs
    // nothing" is a proven fact, and its absence used to read as no answer.
    print("Effects: {show_row_facet(row)}\n")
    match trace {
      Unmarked => (),
      Own => print("Ownership: own\n"),
      Ref => print("Ownership: ref\n"),
    }
    // Each open obligation at the node, said as the ledger says it — a count
    // told the developer that something was owed and not what.
    each(print_obligation, verify)
    // The Teach facet says what it found. It printed one constant sentence
    // for every position the predicate fired at — "an annotation here would
    // unlock a capability", the suggestion itself discarded — so the facet
    // named no annotation and no capability anywhere. A declaration with an
    // authored row is taught the tightening the judgment proved instead.
    match teach {
      TeachAdd(_) => print("Teach: {show_teaching(teach)}\n"),
      TeachAsk(_) => print("Teach: {show_teaching(teach)}\n"),
      TeachNeed(_, _, _) => print("Teach: {show_teaching(teach)}\n"),
      TeachNone => match graph_node_body(h) {
        NStmt(FnStmt(name, _, _, _, _)) => match banked_tightening(name, tightenables()) {
          Some(t) => match t {
            (declared, used) => print(tighten_line(name, declared, used)),
          },
          None => (),
        },
        _ => (),
      },
    }
    // The Why: the node's own chain, then the chain its type is read from
    // when its cell links to another.
    match why {
      [] => (),
      [here, ...types] => {
        print("Why: ")
        render_why_hops(here, target, 0)
        each(render_type_chain(target, ??), types)
      },
    }
  },
}

fn render_type_chain(target: String, chain) = {
  print("     its type: ")
  render_why_hops(chain, target, 0)
}

// A declaration node's own graph cell is a free var (the scheme lives in
// the env — read it there, never render the raw t-var); every other node
// shows its inferred type.
//
// EVERY declaration kind, not just the fn (2026-08-16). The law above was
// written for FnStmt and the other six fell to the catch-all, which is
// exactly the raw t-var the law forbids: a cursor on `handler counter`
// answered `counter : t24295@e21654`, and one on `type Gain = Float where
// self >= 0.0` or `type Coeff = Float repr f64` answered nothing at all.
// Those are the authoring sites of arms 2, 6 and 7, so three of the eight
// could not be read where a developer writes them (PLAN §2 says the eight
// ARE the aspects of one read; PLAN §0 pt 5 says systems explain
// themselves). Each arm below reads the declaration's OWN node — the
// refinement's base and predicate, the alias's target, the handler's
// effect and arm count — never a re-derivation.
fn node_query_line(h, kind) = match graph_node_body(h) {
  NStmt(FnStmt(name, _, _, _, _)) => match env_lookup(name) {
    Some(entry) => match entry {
      (scheme, _, _) => show_type(judgment_ty(scheme)),
    },
    None => show_type(ty_of_kind(h, kind)),
  },
  NStmt(RefineStmt(name, base, pred)) => "{name} = {show_type(base)} where {show_predicate(pred)}",
  NStmt(AliasStmt(name, ty)) => "{name} = {show_type(ty)}",
  NStmt(TypeDefStmt(name, variants)) => "{name} — {int_to_str(len(variants))} variant(s)",
  NStmt(EffectDeclStmt(name, _, ops)) => "effect {name} — {int_to_str(len(ops))} op(s)",
  NStmt(HandlerDeclStmt(name, _, _, state, arms, _)) => "handler {name} — {int_to_str(len(arms))} arm(s), {int_to_str(len(state))} state field(s)",
  NStmt(RowAliasStmt(name, _)) => "{name} — a named effect row",
  NHole(_) => // An UNDETERMINED hole never leaks the raw var — substrate
  // vocabulary at the exact moment the medium should ASK (§1's law: the
  // question beats the guess; the fan cannot enumerate an open type, so
  // the missing constraint IS the answer). A typed hole renders what it
  // DEMANDS — the contract a claim raised over it, refinement and all, or
  // its cell's type where nothing claimed it (the cell holds a shape).
  match hole_demand(h) {
    TVar(_) => "_ — still free: annotate the enclosing function's param or result and the fan arrives with the type",
    ty => show_type(ty),
  },
  NExpr(LitFloat(_)) => // A literal's type IS its body when the cell is still free (a top-level
  // let's value node keeps its own cell unbound; the body already proves
  // the answer — read it, never render the raw t-var).
  "Float",
  NExpr(LitInt(_)) => "Int",
  NExpr(LitString(_)) => "String",
  NExpr(LitBool(_)) => "Bool",
  _ => show_type(ty_of_kind(h, kind)),
}

// The addressed source text itself, read back through the range map — the
// line the user pointed at, sliced to the node's span. The file is the one
// authority on its own text.
// The addressed line's own text. The containment scan that placed a weave
// line inside a module range is gone: the module is the one being addressed
// and the line is its own, so this opens that file and reads that line.
fn source_slice(ref span, ref target: String) = match span {
  Span(sl, sc, el, ec) => {
    let p = driver_module_path(target)
    if fs_exists(p) {
      let text = fs_read_file(p)
      let lstart = nth_line_offset(text, sl - 1, 0, 0)
      let lend = line_end_from(text, lstart)
      let a = lstart + sc - 1
      // A MULTI-LINE span ends on a DIFFERENT line, so its end column is not
      // an offset into this one — slice to the end of the addressed line
      // instead. The end line was discarded here until 2026-08-16, and once
      // declarations carried their true extent a multi-line handler
      // declaration rendered a two-character fragment of its own keyword.
      // A span's end column is EXCLUSIVE and 1-based, so its byte offset is
      // `ec - 1` past the line's start; reading `ec` sliced one character too
      // many — a one-letter variable rendered with the space after it, a
      // callee with its opening parenthesis (measured on boot 8b071ba3).
      let b = if el > sl { lend } else { lstart + ec - 1 }
      let lo = if a < lend { a } else { lstart }
      let hi = if b <= lend { b } else { lend }
      if hi > lo { "{str_slice(text, lo, hi)} : " } else { "" }
    } else {
      ""
    }
  },
}

// Byte offset of the k-th line (0-based) — a forward newline walk.
fn nth_line_offset(ref text, k, i, line) = if line >= k {
  i
} else if i >= byte_len(text) {
  i
} else if byte_at(text, i) == 10 {
  nth_line_offset(text, k, i + 1, line + 1)
} else {
  nth_line_offset(text, k, i + 1, line)
}

fn line_end_from(ref text, i) = if i >= byte_len(text) {
  i
} else if byte_at(text, i) == 10 {
  i
} else {
  line_end_from(text, i + 1)
}

// The Why chain, one hop per line: file:line through the range map, the
// hop's own phrase, and — where the graph carries them — the binding's
// literal value (node_const at the let's value node, read live) and the
// author's attached `//` lede (graph_comment_at). The prose rides the
// same rails as the types: nothing here is stored twice.
fn render_why_hops(reason, target: String, depth) = if depth > 7 {
  print("\n")
} else {
  match reason {
    Located(span, inner) => {
      if depth > 0 { print("     ") } else { () }
      print("{reason_phrase(inner)}, at {file_line(target, span)}{binding_ledes(span, inner)}\n")
      render_why_deeper(inner, target, depth)
    },
    VarLookup(name, br) => {
      if depth > 0 { print("     ") } else { () }
      print("{name} read here\n")
      render_why_hops(br, target, depth + 1)
    },
    _ => {
      if depth > 0 { print("     ") } else { () }
      print("{reason_phrase(reason)}\n")
      render_why_deeper(reason, target, depth)
    },
  }
}

fn render_why_deeper(inner, target: String, depth) = match inner {
  Accepted(_, _, br) => // The accept edge: its phrase names the proposal and its proof; the
  // hop beneath is the binding's own reason, which the acceptance wraps.
  render_why_hops(br, target, depth + 1),
  VarLookup(_, br) => render_why_hops(br, target, depth + 1),
  LetBinding(_, br) => render_why_hops(br, target, depth + 1),
  FnReturn(_, br) => render_why_hops(br, target, depth + 1),
  InferredCallReturn(_, br) => render_why_hops(br, target, depth + 1),
  InferredPipeResult(_, br) => render_why_hops(br, target, depth + 1),
  Unified(a, b) => // A unification joins TWO provenances — walk both (the second indented
  // as its own branch), the DAG rendered as the tree it is.
  {
    render_why_hops(a, target, depth + 1)
    render_why_hops(b, target, depth + 1)
  },
  OpConstraint(_, a, b) => {
    render_why_hops(a, target, depth + 1)
    render_why_hops(b, target, depth + 1)
  },
  IfBranch(br) => render_why_hops(br, target, depth + 1),
  Instantiation(_, br) => render_why_hops(br, target, depth + 1),
  _ => print_string(""),
}

// The value + comment ledes at a binding hop — both read LIVE from the
// graph (the let's value node via node_const; the attached comment via
// the comment weave). Empty strings when the graph carries neither.
fn binding_ledes(span, inner) = match inner {
  LetBinding(_, _) => {
    let spans = span_index_of_snapshot()
    match address_case_a(spans, span_line_of(span), 0, 0, len(spans), None) {
      None => "",
      Some(pick) => match pick {
        (_, sh) => "{value_lede(sh)}{comment_lede(sh)}",
      },
    }
  },
  _ => // drift-audit: ignore — genuine String-null lede: a non-binding hop carries no value/comment lede by construction
  "",
}

fn span_line_of(ref s) with Pure = match s {
  Span(sl, _, _, _) => sl,
}

fn value_lede(sh) = match graph_node_body(sh) {
  NStmt(LetStmt(_, value, _)) => match value {
    N(_, _, vh) => match node_const(vh) {
      Some(LVInt(n)) => " = {int_to_str(n)}",
      Some(LVFloat(f)) => " = {float_to_str(f)}",
      Some(LVString(s)) => " = \"{s}\"",
      Some(LVBool(b)) => if b { " = true" } else { " = false" },
      None => "",
    },
  },
  _ => // drift-audit: ignore — genuine String-null lede: a non-let node has no binding value to render
  "",
}

fn comment_lede(sh) = {
  let c = graph_comment_at(sh)
  if byte_len(c) == 0 { "" } else { ", {comment_first_line(c)}" }
}

fn comment_first_line(ref c) = {
  let cut = addr_newline_at(c, 0, byte_len(c))
  if cut < 0 { c } else { str_slice(c, 0, cut) }
}

fn addr_newline_at(ref s, i, n) =
  if i >= n { 0 - 1 } else if byte_at(s, i) == 10 { i } else { addr_newline_at(s, i + 1, n) }

// ─── test_run — Test effect handler chain
//
// Per EH §1.3 + lib/test.mn: assert / assert_eq / assert_near
// dispatch through assert_reporter (runtime) + verify_assert
// (compile-time lifting). Named follow-up: Hβ.test.lib-test-mn-substrate.
//
// test = the Test effect handler chain over the checked graph. The test
// module is checked (its assertions typecheck against the one graph), then
// its check arms dispatch through assert_reporter — assert/assert_eq are
// the Test effect's ops, the handler decides pass/fail (lib/test.mn: "the
// handler decides what happens"). The reporter is a handler swap: the same
// test source runs under assert_reporter (pass/fail counts) or a benchmark
// handler (timing). Execution of the test fns reaches the process boundary
// (Hβ.cli.process-exec-wire, shared with run); the check + reporter chain
// is the structural form, real now.
fn test_run(target_opt) = {
  let entry_module = match target_opt {
    Some(t) => t,
    None => // default test entry module
    "test",
  }
  // A DIRECTORY target is the BATTERY: every .mn inside is a self-describing
  // gate (its first comment declares `// expect: N` — the expectation lives
  // ON the artifact, SYNTAX §Comments), compiled IN THIS PROCESS with a
  // fresh handler chain per micro (handler state initializes at install —
  // per-micro isolation is the install itself), each WAT written to
  // .build/test/, and a manifest printed for the shim's exec loop (WASI has
  // no process-spawn; the shim owns exec — the one seam). One process
  // replaces one wasmtime invocation per micro.
  // The directory probe and the battery run in the SAME handler extent the
  // single-file sibling installs — the fs probe itself is a WasiFilesystem
  // op, so the bracket must cover the scrutinee too. This install was
  // MISSING (the battery's fs/console ops ran as null-state singletons,
  // reading the null page as their state — tolerated by accident until the
  // SingletonUninstalled guard refused it); test_check_run keeps its own
  // inner chain, which shadows these installs for its extent, unchanged.
  {
    match fs_list_dir_impl(entry_module) {
      Some(names) => battery_run(entry_module, names),
      None => test_check_run(entry_module),
    }
  } ~> stdout_console ~> wasi_filesystem ~> diagnostics_handler
}

fn test_check_run(entry_module) =
  pipeline_check(entry_module) ~> assert_reporter ~> stdout_console ~> wasi_filesystem ~> no_iter_context ~> diagnostics_handler ~> no_state_walked ~> graph_handler([], 0, []) ~> mutate_sink

// ─── The battery: `mentl test <dir>` ────────────────────────────────────
// The micro battery IS the medium reading its own gates: glob the directory,
// read each micro's own `// expect: N` header, compile each against the
// runtime lib set in ONE process, write the WATs, print the manifest. The
// refusal law holds batch-shaped: a micro whose ledger shows errors / holes /
// armed refusals gets a FAILC line and NO WAT (the same
// errors-holes-refusals read `gated_plan` makes for the CLI; here the
// boundary is the batch, so the verdict is a manifest line instead of
// proc_exit).
fn battery_libs() -> String =
  fs_read_file_impl("lib/memory.mn") ++ fs_read_file_impl("lib/strings.mn") ++ fs_read_file_impl("lib/lists.mn") ++ fs_read_file_impl("lib/prelude.mn")

// The fixture's own contract, first line — the expectation lives ON the
// artifact (SYNTAX §Comments), and the grammar mirrors the medium's own
// two verdicts: `// expect: N` (the program compiles and its run answers
// N) and `// expect: refuse E_Class` (the judgment REPORTS the named
// class as an error — armed classes also withhold the artifact, unarmed
// ones surface-and-emit per the sound-incomplete contract; the battery
// judges the JUDGMENT, never stricter than the medium). This contract
// is the frontier bash's dissolution channel: a refusal leg becomes a
// fixture the medium reads itself.
type Expect
  = ExpectRun(Int)
  | ExpectRefuse(String)
  | ExpectPropose(Int, Int, ProposeWant)
  | ExpectTeach(Int, TeachWant)

// A teach fixture's contract (D4): what the gradient answers at the
// declaration on line L — one proven annotation whose source is the text,
// a question naming its alternatives in rank order, a need, or nothing. The
// annotation's own source projection is compared, never the rendered line,
// so the voice may change without a fixture moving.
type TeachWant = WantAdd(String) | WantAskOf(String) | WantNeed | WantNoTeach

// A proposal fixture's contract (C7): what the medium must answer at the
// hole — one proven survivor whose source is the text, a tie whose
// divergence is the named ARM (row · type · name · value · shape ·
// behavior), or no candidate at all. The arm binds the classification,
// never the sentence, so the voice may change without a fixture moving.
type ProposeWant = WantFill(String) | WantAsk(String) | WantNone

// The micro's own expectation: first line `// expect: N`,
// `// expect: refuse E_Class`, or `// propose L:C: fill <text>` |
// `// propose L:C: ask <arm>` | `// propose L:C: none`. None = not a
// battery member (skipped loudly by the manifest consumer).
fn battery_expect(src) = {
  let prefix = "// expect: "
  if byte_len(src) >= 11 && str_eq(str_slice(src, 0, 11), "// propose ") {
    battery_propose_expect(src)
  } else if byte_len(src) >= 9 && str_eq(str_slice(src, 0, 9), "// teach ") {
    battery_teach_expect(src)
  } else if byte_len(src) < 12 {
    None
  } else if !str_eq(str_slice(src, 0, 11), prefix) {
    None
  } else {
    if byte_len(src) >= 18 && str_eq(str_slice(src, 11, 18), "refuse ") {
      Some(ExpectRefuse(battery_expect_word(src, 18, 18)))
    } else {
      Some(ExpectRun(battery_expect_digits(src, 11, 0)))
    }
  }
}

// The class name: bytes from `start` to the first whitespace/newline.
fn battery_expect_word(src, start, i) = if i >= byte_len(src) {
  str_slice(src, start, i)
} else {
  let b = byte_at(src, i)
  if b == 10 || b == 13 || b == 32 {
    str_slice(src, start, i)
  } else {
    battery_expect_word(src, start, i + 1)
  }
}

fn battery_expect_digits(src, i, acc) = if i >= byte_len(src) {
  acc
} else {
  let b = byte_at(src, i)
  if b >= 48 && b <= 57 { battery_expect_digits(src, i + 1, acc * 10 + (b - 48)) } else { acc }
}

// `// propose L:C: <want>` — the address, then the want: `fill` takes the
// rest of the line (a candidate's source may carry spaces), `ask` one word
// (the arm), `none` nothing.
fn battery_propose_expect(src) = {
  let line = battery_expect_digits(src, 11, 0)
  let col_start = battery_skip_digits(src, 11) + 1
  let col = battery_expect_digits(src, col_start, 0)
  let word_start = battery_skip_digits(src, col_start) + 2
  let word = battery_expect_word(src, word_start, word_start)
  let rest_start = word_start + byte_len(word) + 1
  if str_eq(word, "fill") {
    Some(ExpectPropose(line, col, WantFill(battery_line_rest(src, rest_start, rest_start))))
  } else if str_eq(word, "ask") {
    Some(ExpectPropose(line, col, WantAsk(battery_expect_word(src, rest_start, rest_start))))
  } else if str_eq(word, "none") {
    Some(ExpectPropose(line, col, WantNone))
  } else {
    None
  }
}

// `// teach L: <want>` — the declaration's line, then the want: `add`
// takes the rest of the line (an annotation's source carries spaces),
// `ask` the alternatives joined by ` or `, `need` and `none` nothing.
fn battery_teach_expect(src) = {
  let line = battery_expect_digits(src, 9, 0)
  let word_start = battery_skip_digits(src, 9) + 2
  let word = battery_expect_word(src, word_start, word_start)
  let rest_start = word_start + byte_len(word) + 1
  if str_eq(word, "add") {
    Some(ExpectTeach(line, WantAdd(battery_line_rest(src, rest_start, rest_start))))
  } else if str_eq(word, "ask") {
    Some(ExpectTeach(line, WantAskOf(battery_line_rest(src, rest_start, rest_start))))
  } else if str_eq(word, "need") {
    Some(ExpectTeach(line, WantNeed))
  } else if str_eq(word, "none") {
    Some(ExpectTeach(line, WantNoTeach))
  } else {
    None
  }
}

fn battery_skip_digits(src, i) = if i >= byte_len(src) {
  i
} else {
  let b = byte_at(src, i)
  if b >= 48 && b <= 57 { battery_skip_digits(src, i + 1) } else { i }
}

// The bytes from `start` to the end of the line.
fn battery_line_rest(src, start, i) = if i >= byte_len(src) {
  str_slice(src, start, i)
} else {
  let b = byte_at(src, i)
  if b == 10 || b == 13 { str_slice(src, start, i) } else { battery_line_rest(src, start, i + 1) }
}

// THE PROPOSAL BATTERY (C7): the fixture is judged at its hole through the
// same address resolution and the same eight-aspect view the CLI's
// `mentl <file>:<line>:<col>` builds, and the contract is compared to the
// Verdict itself — structurally, never through the rendered line.
fn battery_propose_step(stem, path: String, line, col, want) = match battery_view(path, line, col) {
  None => {
    print_string("FAIL(propose) {stem}: no node at {path}:{int_to_str(line)}:{int_to_str(col)}\n")
    1
  },
  Some(view) => match view {
    CursorView(_, propose, _, _, _, _, _, _) => match propose {
      Proposal(p) => if verdict_meets(want, p.verdict) {
        print_string("PASS {stem}: propose {show_want(want)}\n")
        0
      } else {
        print_string("FAIL(propose) {stem}: wanted {show_want(want)}, got {verdict_word(p.verdict)}\n")
        1
      },
    },
  },
}

// THE TEACH BATTERY (D4): the fixture is judged at its declaration's line
// through the address route and the eight-aspect view, and the contract is
// compared to the gradient's Teaching itself.
fn battery_teach_step(stem, path: String, line, want) = match battery_view(path, line, 0) {
  None => {
    print_string("FAIL(teach) {stem}: no node at {path}:{int_to_str(line)}\n")
    1
  },
  Some(view) => match view {
    CursorView(_, _, _, _, _, _, teach, _) => if teach_meets(want, teach) {
      print_string("PASS {stem}: teach {show_teach_want(want)}\n")
      0
    } else {
      print_string("FAIL(teach) {stem}: wanted {show_teach_want(want)}, got {show_teaching(teach)}\n")
      1
    },
  },
}

// The eight-aspect view at a fixture's address — the one read both
// contracts judge, built as the CLI's `mentl <file>:<line>:<col>` builds it.
fn battery_view(path: String, line, col) = infer_context(() => match driver_entry_with_ranges(path) {
  (_ast, _ranges) => match address_pick(path, line, col) {
    None => None,
    Some(pick) => match pick {
      (_pspan, ph) => Some(cursor_at_handle(ph)),
    },
  },
} ~> cursor_default ~> synth_default ~> interrogate_default ~> mentl_default ~> tighten_collector)

fn teach_meets(want, teach) = match want {
  WantAdd(text) => match teach {
    TeachAdd(s) => str_eq(suggestion_text(s), text),
    TeachAsk(_) => false,
    TeachNeed(_, _, _) => false,
    TeachNone => false,
  },
  WantAskOf(texts) => match teach {
    TeachAsk(ss) => str_eq(join(" or ", map(suggestion_text, ss)), texts),
    TeachAdd(_) => false,
    TeachNeed(_, _, _) => false,
    TeachNone => false,
  },
  WantNeed => match teach {
    TeachNeed(_, _, _) => true,
    TeachAdd(_) => false,
    TeachAsk(_) => false,
    TeachNone => false,
  },
  WantNoTeach => match teach {
    TeachNone => true,
    TeachAdd(_) => false,
    TeachAsk(_) => false,
    TeachNeed(_, _, _) => false,
  },
}

fn suggestion_text(s) = match s {
  AnnotationSuggestion(ann, _, _) => show_annotation(ann),
}

fn show_teach_want(want) = match want {
  WantAdd(text) => "add {text}",
  WantAskOf(texts) => "ask {texts}",
  WantNeed => "need",
  WantNoTeach => "none",
}

fn verdict_meets(want, verdict) = match want {
  WantNone => match verdict {
    VNone => true,
    VFill(_, _) => false,
    VAsk(_, _) => false,
  },
  WantFill(text) => match verdict {
    VFill(node, _) => str_eq(render_candidate_source(node), text),
    VNone => false,
    VAsk(_, _) => false,
  },
  WantAsk(arm) => match verdict {
    VAsk(d, _) => str_eq(divergence_arm(d), arm),
    VNone => false,
    VFill(_, _) => false,
  },
}

fn verdict_word(verdict) = match verdict {
  VNone => "none",
  VFill(node, _) => "fill {render_candidate_source(node)}",
  VAsk(d, survivors) => "ask {divergence_arm(d)} ({int_to_str(len(survivors))} survivors)",
}

fn show_want(want) = match want {
  WantNone => "none",
  WantFill(text) => "fill {text}",
  WantAsk(arm) => "ask {arm}",
}

// What one micro's compile left behind: the gate withheld the module, the
// artifact could not be opened (a host fault, never a verdict on the
// program), or the module is on disk for the host to run.
type BatteryEmit = BatteryRefused | BatteryUnwritable | BatteryWritten

fn battery_wat_path(stem) = ".build/battery/{stem}.wat"

// One micro, one fresh chain — the same chain compile_stdin drives, with
// the emission STREAMED through wat_to_file into the battery's artifact
// directory (the module's text is never held whole — the scope at
// wat_to_string's decl). The HOST runs it: for a run contract the battery
// prints a RUN line naming the module and what it must answer, and the
// command that invoked `mentl test` assembles each one, executes it and
// judges the exit (tools/wt-env.sh, wt_battery) — the compiler reached
// directly can only emit, in the battery exactly as at `mentl run`.
// Returns (what the compile left, error-count, banked classes).
fn battery_compile(src, stem) = compile_context(() => {
  let lowered =
    src |> infer_program_converged |> saturate_pass |> lower_program |> reachable_from_main
  // THE SAME law the CLI compile applies, batch-shaped: refusal = holes +
  // ARMED classes only, the plan's settle point included. Unarmed errors
  // surface on stderr and the compile proceeds — the sound-incomplete
  // contract; the battery must not be stricter than the medium (it FAILC'd
  // ev2 for an unarmed row mismatch the check verb tolerates). `gated_plan`
  // is the law's one home; this policy counts and continues where the CLI
  // compile proc_exits. The refuse contract judges the JUDGMENT through the
  // collector's bank (mcp_diag_collector, the forwarder: every line still
  // reaches stderr).
  let left = match gated_plan(lowered) {
    Withheld(_, _) => BatteryRefused,
    Planned(plan) => {
      let fd = fs_create(battery_wat_path(stem))
      if fd == 0 {
        BatteryUnwritable
      } else {
        {
          emit_planned(plan)
        } ~> wat_to_file(fd)
        fs_close(fd)
        BatteryWritten
      }
    },
  }
  // THE CLASSES, never the rendered lines: the refuse contract compares
  // names, so no substring scan over prose decides a verdict.
  let classes = gate_classes()
  let nerr = diag_errors()
  (left, nerr, classes)
} ~> mcp_diag_collector ~> wasi_filesystem)

fn battery_run(dir, names) = {
  // The libs and the fixture names are flat before the first micro: each
  // compile reads `libs ++ src` and indexes the names, and a flat sequence
  // answers both in one step.
  let libs = str_concat("", battery_libs())
  let micros = list_to_flat(sort(filter(ends_with(??, ".mn"), names)))
  // The artifact directory exists before the first module is written; a
  // directory already there answers the same (EEXIST is absorbed). The op,
  // never its impl: the row names what the battery does to the host.
  {
    fs_mkdir(".build")
    fs_mkdir(".build/battery")
  } ~> wasi_filesystem
  battery_loop(dir, libs, micros, 0, 0)
}

// Each micro's compile is an ARENA (lib/arena.mn): what the compile
// allocated is dead once its verdict is printed, except what it stored into
// memory older than the micro, so the battery's peak is one compile's live
// set, never the sum, and a micro's output cannot depend on the micros
// before it unless something it stored outlived it — which the arena's
// census counts as moved.
fn battery_loop(dir: String, libs, micros: [String], i, fails) = if i >= len(micros) {
  fails
} else {
  let step = battery_step(dir, libs, micros[i]) ~> arena
  battery_loop(dir, libs, micros, i + 1, fails + step)
}

fn battery_step(dir: String, libs, name: String) = {
  let stem = str_slice(name, 0, byte_len(name) - 3)
  let src = fs_read_file_impl("{dir}/{name}")
  match battery_expect(src) {
    None => {
      print_string("NOEXPECT {stem}\n")
      1
    },
    Some(contract) => match contract {
      ExpectPropose(line, col, want) => // The driver takes the MODULE path — the file without its `.mn`, exactly
      // as the CLI's address form hands it over (path_to_module, cli.mn).
      battery_propose_step(stem, path_to_module("{dir}/{name}"), line, col, want),
      ExpectTeach(line, want) => battery_teach_step(stem, path_to_module("{dir}/{name}"), line, want),
      ExpectRun(want) => match battery_compile(libs ++ src, stem) {
        (left, nerr, _) => match left {
          BatteryRefused => {
            print_string("FAILC {stem}\n")
            1
          },
          BatteryUnwritable => {
            print_string("FAIL(write) {stem}: cannot open {battery_wat_path(stem)}\n")
            1
          },
          BatteryWritten => // The verdict is the host's: the RUN line hands over the module,
          // the exit it must answer and the error COUNT (a fixture that
          // runs true while reporting errors is the sound-incomplete
          // contract at work, and a count nobody prints is a count nobody
          // reads); the command that invoked the battery runs the module
          // and prints PASS or FAIL(run) in this line's place.
          {
            print_string("RUN {stem} {battery_wat_path(stem)} {int_to_str(want)} {int_to_str(nerr)}\n")
            0
          },
        },
      },
      ExpectRefuse(cls) => match battery_compile(libs ++ src, stem) {
        (_, nerr, classes) => // The contract judges the JUDGMENT: the named class reported as an
        // error. Armed classes also withhold the artifact; unarmed ones
        // surface-and-emit — both are the medium's own law, and the
        // battery asserts exactly what the medium asserts.
        if nerr > 0 && any((c) => c == cls, classes) {
          print_string("REFUSE {stem} {cls}\n")
          0
        } else {
          // The verdict names what the judgment DID bank, so a contract that
          // misses says which class stood where the wanted one was expected.
          print_string("FAILR {stem} wanted {cls} (errors={int_to_str(nerr)}; banked: {join(", ", classes)})\n")
          1
        },
      },
    },
  }
}

// assert_reporter — the Test effect's pass/fail reporter handler. Each
// check(cond, msg) folds into (passed, failed); get_results projects the
// tally. The handler IS the test framework (no framework needed —
// lib/test.mn). State threads the counts; a failing assertion surfaces its
// message at the WASI boundary.
handler assert_reporter with passed = 0, failed = 0 {
  check(cond, msg) => if cond {
    resume() with passed = passed + 1
  } else {
    eprint_string("  FAIL: {msg}\n")
    resume() with failed = failed + 1
  },
  get_results() => resume((passed, failed)),
}

// ─── audit_run — capability set + severance suggestions
//
// Walks the ~> chain; collects effect rows transitively; emits
// AuditReport. Per DESIGN §9.1 + traces/a-day §1400.
fn audit_run(target) = {
  audit(target) ~> stdout_console ~> wasi_filesystem ~> mentl_default ~> verify_ledger ~> diagnostics_handler ~> no_state_walked ~> graph_handler([], 0, []) ~> mutate_sink
  0
}

// ─── query_run — structured ad-hoc query
//
// `mentl query <module> "<question>"` — read-only query against the
// substrate. A DIRECTORY is a tree of independent programs, each its own
// link as `mentl test <dir>` runs them, and a site facet answers across all
// of them (`query_dir`). The directory probe is a filesystem op, so the
// bracket covers it, as the battery's does.
fn query_run(target, question) = match fs_list_dir_impl(target) {
  Some(names) => query_dir(dir_base(target), names, question),
  None => query(target, question),
} ~> stdout_console ~> wasi_filesystem ~> mentl_default ~> diagnostics_handler ~> no_state_walked ~> graph_handler([], 0, []) ~> mutate_sink

// Every program in the directory judged on its own fresh chain, each inside
// an arena so the walk's peak is one judgment's live set, and asked the
// question; the sites in each program's own module are the answer. A facet
// that answers anything but sites refuses, naming the ones that do.
fn query_dir(dir: String, names, question) = {
  let programs = list_to_flat(sort(filter(ends_with(??, ".mn"), names)))
  let answers = map(query_program(dir, question, ??), programs)
  if any(answer_is_not_sites, answers) {
    eprint_string("mentl query: a directory answers the site facets — refs of NAME, census SHAPE, text NEEDLE, prose NEEDLE, decls\n")
    1
  } else {
    let found = flat_map(answer_sites, answers)
    let unread = len(filter(answer_is_unread, answers))
    if unread > 0 {
      eprint_string("mentl query: {int_to_str(unread)} program(s) in {dir} never joined the weave\n")
    } else {
      ()
    }
    println("→ {int_to_str(len(found))} {fold("site(s)", answer_label, answers)} across {int_to_str(len(programs))} program(s){render_census_sites(found)}")
    0
  }
}

fn query_program(dir: String, question, name: String) =
  query_sites(path_to_module("{dir}/{name}"), question) ~> arena

fn dir_base(dir: String) =
  if ends_with(dir, "/") { str_slice(dir, 0, byte_len(dir) - 1) } else { dir }

fn answer_is_not_sites(answer) = match answer {
  Some(found) => match found {
    Some(_) => false,
    None => true,
  },
  None => false,
}

fn answer_is_unread(answer) = match answer {
  Some(_) => false,
  None => true,
}

fn answer_sites(answer) = match answer {
  Some(found) => match found {
    Some(pair) => match pair {
      (_, sites) => sites,
    },
    None => [],
  },
  None => [],
}

fn answer_label(label, answer) = match answer {
  Some(found) => match found {
    Some(pair) => match pair {
      (l, _) => l,
    },
    None => label,
  },
  None => label,
}

// ─── verify_run — the medium judges its own source (`mentl verify`)
//
// Reads every bound in `board_bounds()` against the count `census_sites`
// computes live, on ONE judged graph, and refuses when any is breached.
//
// THE MEASUREMENT THAT MADE THIS A LANDING AND NOT A PORT: the shell gate
// answered these same twelve counts with twelve separate invocations of the
// medium — `query src/main.mn "census eta"`, then `"census
// effectful-lambda"`, then nine drift shapes, then the open receiver — each
// one a whole wheel compile thrown away after a regex read one integer out
// of its rendering. Twelve judgments of one program to ask twelve questions
// of one graph. The verb judges once and reads twelve columns off it, which
// is the Carried-Truth Law at the gate layer: the graph already knew.
//
// The board REFUSES rather than reports. A breached bound exits nonzero
// because a gate that narrates is a gate that stops being run (§11 tripwire
// 4), and the one thing this verb may never do is let a rise through with a
// note about it.
// THE WEAVE JUDGMENT, not `pipeline_check`, and this verb learned it the way
// `mentl query` did — by measuring twelve confident zeros on its first run.
// `pipeline_check` judges without leaving the weave the census walks, so
// every shape counted nothing and the board reported twelve bounds holding
// on a graph that held no source. That is `Hβ.query.unreadable-source-
// refusal` reproduced at a second surface, and the fix is its own ruling: a
// board over an unread weave REFUSES the question rather than answering it,
// because the one failure a gate may never have is a green that means
// "nothing was measured" (§11 tripwire 4 — a gate that stops being reported
// stops being run; a gate that measures nothing has stopped being run while
// still being reported).
fn verify_run(target_opt) = infer_context(() => {
  let entry_module = match target_opt {
    Some(t) => t,
    None => "main",
  }
  // SCOPE ALL, because this is the BOARD — the medium's standing bounds on
  // its own source, not a user reading one file. Every other entry narrows to
  // the module the user named so a compile never buries them in prelude
  // narration; the board is the one reader for which that narrowing is the
  // defect, and while it inherited the narrowing the comment-reference count
  // could only ever see the entry module's prose (measured 2026-09-20:
  // 0 on the entry link against 58 across the modules).
  match driver_entry_scoped(entry_module, ScopeAll) {
    (_ast, ranges) => match range_of_module(ranges, entry_module) {
      None => {
        eprint_string("mentl verify: no source to judge — {entry_module} never joined the weave\n")
        1
      },
      Some(_) => // AND THE WEAVE MUST HAVE JUDGED CLEAN, which is the same vacuity one
      // layer in and the instrument gate is what found it: a module whose own
      // file loads but whose IMPORT does not still joins the weave, so the
      // arm above admits it, the census walks a graph missing that module's
      // content, and every bound reads a number too small to breach. It
      // answered "12 bound(s) hold" for a program the driver had just
      // refused. A partial measurement is worse than none because it looks
      // like one, so the board reads the judgment's own error count and
      // declines to measure rather than measuring less.
      if diag_errors() > 0 {
        eprint_string("mentl verify: {entry_module} did not judge clean — a board over a refused weave measures a program nobody has\n")
        1
      } else {
        verify_board(entry_module)
      },
    },
  }
} ~> stdout_console ~> wasi_filesystem ~> mentl_default)

fn verify_board(entry_module: String) = {
  let board = board_read()
  print_string("verify: the medium's verdict on {entry_module}\n")
  each(print_board_line, board)
  let breached = len(filter(board_breached, board))
  if breached > 0 {
    print_string("verify: {int_to_str(breached)} bound(s) breached — a count the medium keeps about itself rose.\n")
    1
  } else {
    print_string("verify: {int_to_str(len(board))} bound(s) hold.\n")
    0
  }
}

fn print_board_line(entry) = print_string(board_line(entry))

// ─── with_run — the universal escape hatch (env_lookup resolution)
//
// `mentl --with <handler> [path]` installs an arbitrary user-defined
// entry-handler resolved by env_lookup — the SAME mechanism every
// built-in verb uses (the env IS the dispatch table; the type system IS
// the package manager, §5). The handler name resolves against the
// checked program's env; a built-in and a user verb are one lookup.
// Resolution of the handle into the install site is the named peer
// Hβ.cli.user-defined-entry-handler-resolution — the env overlay it
// reads is the per-module env the driver populates (driver IC.3 chase).
fn with_run(handler_name, target_opt) = {
  let entry_module = match target_opt {
    Some(t) => t,
    None => "main",
  }
  pipeline_check(entry_module)
  match env_lookup(handler_name) {
    None => eprint_string("mentl --with: no handler named {handler_name} in the checked env\n"),
    Some(_) => eprint_string("mentl --with {handler_name}: resolved; install via Hβ.cli.user-defined-entry-handler-resolution\n"),
  }
  // The verb is named but its install path is unbuilt — the CLI refusal
  // law: a named verb that cannot do its work exits nonzero.
  1
}

// ─── teach_run — terminal voice surface (default)
//
// Bare `mentl` defaults here per EH. Gradient narration; one
// suggestion per turn; Mentl's voice in the terminal for
// vim/helix/terminal-only users.
fn teach_run(target_opt) = {
  let entry_module = match target_opt {
    Some(t) => t,
    None => "main",
  }
  // teach reaches inference — the core installs as the one bracket (this
  // chain was the eighth found missing the analysis handlers, by design
  // pass not by trap; query_default's env_lookup / env_snapshot resolve
  // into the bracket's env).
  infer_context(() => teach(entry_module) ~> stdout_console ~> wasi_filesystem ~> mentl_voice_default ~>
  // mentl_voice_default's consult/run_query bubble Query.ask here
  query_default)
  0
}

// ─── repl_run — the line-eval cursor
//
// The REPL IS the cursor reading one line at a time: each line is a
// frontend |> infer projection, the result spoken through the voice
// surface (the same teach projection, scoped to one entry). It is the
// `<~` edit loop (cursor_transport) with the line as the caret event —
// one cursor, the terminal transport, fed line-by-line. The stdin line
// reader is the host boundary (Hβ.repl.line-read-wire, peer of the
// process/web/nvim wires); the eval-and-project chain is real now.
fn repl_run() = {
  // The line-eval runs infer; the core installs as the one bracket (the
  // driverless-zero-state class the bounds trap unmasked lives in its law).
  infer_context(() => repl_eval_line(read_stdin_line()) ~> stdout_console ~> wasi_filesystem ~> mentl_voice_default ~> query_default)
  0
}

// repl_eval_line — one cursor read of a source line: lex |> parse |> infer,
// then project the inferred shape through the voice surface. Empty line is
// the empty projection (the cursor at rest), never a fabricated value.
fn repl_eval_line(line) = if byte_len(line) == 0 {
  ""
} else {
  infer_program_converged(line)
  render_query_result(ask(parse_query_string("", "type main")))
}

// ─── new — the welcome's own WRITE
//
// The welcome PROJECTS a directory (project_directory, the READ); `new`
// draws the first edge into it (the WRITE) and then re-projects. One
// graph, two operations, at the shell — `new` is the second operation,
// never a scaffolding engine.
//
// ONE FILE, never a tree. A project directory exists to hold a manifest,
// and the imports ARE the manifest (PLAN §11's novelty audit); a `.mn`
// file carrying `fn main()` IS an executable (SYNTAX §Top-level program
// structure), so `hello.mn` beside you IS the project — and `mentl run
// hello` reaches it through the resolver's own first arm (driver.mn's
// direct read). `{name}/src/main.mn` was Cargo's shape borrowed one level
// down, and the resolver could not even see it.
//
// The seed is a string the medium CARRIES (new_seed). Reading it from a
// repo file made the CLI depend on an artifact outside itself: from any
// directory but the repo, fs_read_file found nothing, returned "" (its
// own surrender-fallback) and `new` wrote a ZERO-BYTE file while printing
// "scaffolded ... from lib/tutorial/00-hello.mn" — a claim about the
// world that was false.
//
// So `new` states nothing it did not read. It never claims "wrote
// hello.mn"; it re-projects the directory and lets the file speak for
// itself — a write followed by the live read is unfalsifiable, and it is
// the same `<~` the cursor session runs (draw the edge, re-project).
fn new_project(name) = {
  scaffold_project(name) ~> wasi_filesystem ~> no_iter_context ~> diagnostics_handler ~> no_state_walked ~> graph_handler([], 0, []) ~> mutate_sink
  0
}

// String pins on this and the sibling verb/render fns below are Intent
// Boundaries (spec_mangle's law): a param whose only use is a splice
// generalizes, and the word-floor copy renders it as a pointer numeral.
fn scaffold_project(name: String) = {
  let dest = "{name}.mn"
  if fs_exists(dest) {
    // Refusal teaches: the medium never overwrites what it did not write.
    eprint_string("mentl new {name}: {dest} is already here\n\n")
    eprint_string("the medium never overwrites what it did not write.\n")
    eprint_string("  read it:      mentl edit {name}\n")
    eprint_string("  or name another:  mentl new {name}2\n")
    1
  } else {
    fs_write_file(dest, new_seed())
    project_directory()
    0
  }
}

// The seed the medium carries — the smallest program that RUNS and whose
// own comments are the Reason edges it talks about. Not the curriculum
// (lib/tutorial/ is the curriculum, and it is not on a user's disk).
// The `\{who\}` escapes emit literal braces, so the FILE gets a live
// interpolation the user's own compile reads.
fn new_seed() =
  "// this file is the whole project — the imports are the manifest.\n//\n// the comment you are reading is not a remark the lexer discards: it is\n// a Reason edge in the graph. ask the medium about this file:\n//   mentl audit   — what it is allowed to do\n//   mentl edit    — the cursor, and what it proposes\n\nimport io\n\n// `|>` is the sequential verb — the string flows into print_string.\n// the topology is on the page; there is no hidden control flow.\nfn greet(who) = \"hello, \{who\} — the medium is reading you back\\n\" |> print_string\n\nfn main() = \{\n  greet(\"world\")\n  0\n\}\n"

// ─── help + error surface ─────────────────────────────────────────
fn print_error_and_help(msg) = {
  eprint_string("mentl: ")
  eprint_string(msg)
  eprint_string("\n\n")
  print_help()
  // A malformed invocation exits 2 (the usage-error convention the gate
  // scripts already speak) — main's value is the process exit code.
  2
}

// The verb catalog — ONE surface projected from the verb table, so the help a
// user reads can never disagree with the parser that reads them. The bare
// `mentl` line (the directory projection, not a named verb) frames the list;
// every verb line is verb_specs()[i] rendered uniformly.
fn verb_catalog() =
  "the verbs:\n  mentl                          — project this directory (a pipe compiles stdin)\n" ++ catalog_lines(verb_specs(), 0, len(verb_specs()), "") ++ "\na target is a module name or a file path, from any directory.\n" ++ "the surface: PLAN.md §11 (the production bar) · docs/SYNTAX.md (the language)\n"

// One catalog line per spec: `  mentl <name> <hint>` padded to a column, then
// `— <desc>`. The `++` accumulator IS the O(1) concat rope (§4①), not a loop
// re-alloc — the string grows by pointer, never by copy.
fn catalog_lines(specs, i, n, acc) = if i >= n {
  acc
} else {
  match specs[i] {
    VerbSpec(name, hint, _, desc) => {
      let usage = if byte_len(hint) > 0 { name ++ " " ++ hint } else { name }
      catalog_lines(specs, i + 1, n, acc ++ "  mentl " ++ pad_right(usage, 25) ++ "— " ++ desc ++ "\n")
    },
  }
}

// pad_right — right-pad with spaces to width w (the alignment column). A no-op
// once s already reaches w. Small, bounded, one home for the help alignment.
fn pad_right(s, w) = if byte_len(s) >= w { s } else { pad_right(s ++ " ", w) }

fn print_help() = eprint_string(verb_catalog())

// ─── the welcome — bare `mentl` in a terminal, `mentl help` ────────
//
// Not a manpage: a PROJECTION of where you are (the cursor law at the
// shell). The medium reads the current directory through the preopen,
// names the .mn sources it sees, and offers each one's next keystroke;
// an empty directory gets the beginning. stdout, exit 0 — help asked
// for is never an error (the ERROR surface, print_error_and_help,
// stays on stderr).
fn welcome_run() = {
  print_string("mentl — the medium between intent and execution\n")
  print_string("any intelligence may propose; nothing executes unproven\n\n")
  project_directory()
  print_string(verb_catalog())
  0
}

// project_directory — the cursor law at the shell: read where you ARE and
// name each source's next keystroke. The welcome opens with it; `new`
// closes with it (which is why `new` never has to claim what it wrote —
// the projection reads the live directory back). An empty directory gets
// the beginning, so the surface is never a dead end.
fn project_directory() = {
  let sources = filter((n) => ends_with(n, ".mn"), fs_list_dir_names())
  if len(sources) > 0 {
    print_string("in this directory:\n")
    each(
      (n) => {
        let stem = str_slice(n, 0, byte_len(n) - 3)
        print_string("  {n}  →  mentl run {stem} · mentl edit {stem} · mentl check {stem}\n")
      },
      sources
    )
    print_string("\n")
  } else {
    print_string("nothing to read here yet — begin:\n  mentl new hello && mentl run hello\n\n")
  }
}
